  ($qb$S02C08Q09$qb$, $qb$S02$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Employee dignity during exit processes (resignation, redundancy, dismissal) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Preserved — exits are handled with respect, professionalism, and appropriate support regardless of circumstances$qb$, $qb$Generally preserved — most exits are handled adequately$qb$, $qb$Variable — exit quality depends on the relationship between manager and employee$qb$, $qb$Poor — exits in this organisation are handled in ways that damage the departing person's dignity$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S02C08Q10$qb$, $qb$S02$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Senior leadership in your organisation models the respectful management behaviour they expect from others:$qb$, $qb$Consistently — senior leaders are known for treating everyone with dignity regardless of level$qb$, $qb$Mostly — senior leaders generally model respect but occasional lapses are visible$qb$, $qb$Inconsistently — some senior leaders model respect; others visibly do not$qb$, $qb$No — senior leadership behaviour in terms of respect is itself a problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S02C09Q01$qb$, $qb$S02$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$When management in your organisation commits to a timeline or deliverable, the typical outcome is:$qb$, $qb$Delivered on time — management commitments are treated as binding and are reliably fulfilled$qb$, $qb$Mostly delivered — most commitments are met; some slip with acknowledgement$qb$, $qb$Inconsistently delivered — a pattern of partial delivery has eroded confidence in management commitments$qb$, $qb$Rarely delivered — management commitments are treated as aspirational and are not expected to be kept$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S02C09Q02$qb$, $qb$S02$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$Organisational commitments made by management around employee development (training, promotion, role changes) are:$qb$, $qb$Honoured — development commitments are tracked and delivered$qb$, $qb$Mostly honoured — significant development commitments are kept; smaller ones sometimes slip$qb$, $qb$Frequently not honoured — development commitments are made but rarely result in action$qb$, $qb$Systematically broken — employees have stopped expecting management development commitments to materialise$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S02C09Q03$qb$, $qb$S02$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$When management in your organisation cannot keep a commitment due to changed circumstances, they:$qb$, $qb$Proactively communicate — they inform the affected party early, explain why, and propose alternatives$qb$, $qb$Reactively acknowledge — they admit the miss when it becomes apparent$qb$, $qb$Silently drop it — commitments that cannot be kept are simply not fulfilled without explanation$qb$, $qb$Deny it — the original commitment is not acknowledged$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S02C09Q04$qb$, $qb$S02$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$E$qb$, $qb$Salary and compensation commitments made by management in your organisation during hiring, appraisals, or performance reviews are:$qb$, $qb$Delivered as committed — compensation commitments are honoured accurately and on time$qb$, $qb$Generally delivered — occasional discrepancies are acknowledged and corrected$qb$, $qb$Frequently delayed or reduced — compensation commitments are not reliably delivered$qb$, $qb$Routinely broken — broken compensation commitments are a known and significant employee issue$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S02C09Q05$qb$, $qb$S02$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Strategic commitments made to clients, partners, or investors in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Kept — external commitments are treated with the highest level of seriousness$qb$, $qb$Mostly kept — significant external commitments are honoured; some details slip$qb$, $qb$Inconsistently kept — external parties have experienced delivery failures$qb$, $qb$Frequently not kept — delivery failure with external parties is a known and damaging pattern$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S02C09Q06$qb$, $qb$S02$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$Changes to working conditions, policies, or benefits that were promised or implied during recruitment in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Maintained — what was communicated during recruitment is what employees experience$qb$, $qb$Mostly maintained — significant conditions are kept; some implied benefits have not materialised$qb$, $qb$Partially maintained — notable gaps exist between recruitment promises and employment reality$qb$, $qb$Not maintained — the gap between what was promised and what is delivered is a known retention problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S02C09Q07$qb$, $qb$S02$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Board and investor commitments made by your organisation's leadership are:$qb$, $qb$Met or proactively renegotiated with honesty — leadership maintains credibility with its governance stakeholders$qb$, $qb$Mostly met — some slippage but overall credibility is maintained$qb$, $qb$Inconsistently met — there is a pattern of commitment slippage with governance stakeholders$qb$, $qb$Frequently missed — leadership credibility with the board or investors is a significant concern$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S02C09Q08$qb$, $qb$S02$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$When a management promise affects a team's workload, resources, or deliverables in your organisation and is then broken, the impact is:$qb$, $qb$Managed — management acknowledges the impact and provides compensating support$qb$, $qb$Acknowledged but not fully compensated — teams bear the cost of broken promises without full support$qb$, $qb$Ignored — teams are expected to absorb the impact of broken management commitments without acknowledgement$qb$, $qb$Compounded — broken promises are followed by additional pressure rather than support$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S02C09Q09$qb$, $qb$S02$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The track record of management keeping commitments in your organisation has created a culture where:$qb$, $qb$Commitments are taken seriously — being committed to something means it will be delivered$qb$, $qb$Most commitments are taken seriously — employees trust major commitments but are sceptical of smaller ones$qb$, $qb$Commitment fatigue exists — employees no longer react significantly to management commitments$qb$, $qb$Cynicism is dominant — management commitments are widely treated as aspirational statements with low probability of delivery$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S02C09Q10$qb$, $qb$S02$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Accountability mechanisms in your organisation for management failing to deliver on commitments are:$qb$, $qb$Clear and applied — failure to deliver on commitments has visible and consistent consequences$qb$, $qb$Partial — serious failures are addressed; smaller ones are not$qb$, $qb$Weak — accountability for broken commitments exists in theory but rarely in practice$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — there are no meaningful consequences for management failing to deliver on commitments$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S02C10Q01$qb$, $qb$S02$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$When commercial pressure is intense in your organisation, management's ethical standards:$qb$, $qb$Hold firm — integrity is maintained regardless of the commercial cost$qb$, $qb$Generally hold — occasional compromises are made but are acknowledged as exceptions$qb$, $qb$Bend — ethical standards consistently loosen when commercial pressure is high$qb$, $qb$Collapse — under real pressure, ethical constraints are routinely discarded$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S02C10Q02$qb$, $qb$S02$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$When management in your organisation faces a decision that sits in a grey area (not clearly right or wrong), the decision-making process typically involves:$qb$, $qb$Reference to values and principles — grey area decisions are explicitly tested against organisational values$qb$, $qb$Consultation — grey area decisions are discussed with others before being made$qb$, $qb$Expediency — grey area decisions default to what is most convenient$qb$, $qb$Avoidance — grey area decisions are deferred or delegated to avoid responsibility$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S02C10Q03$qb$, $qb$S02$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Management in your organisation uses organisational resources (people, budget, assets) with the same care whether or not they are being monitored:$qb$, $qb$Yes — resource stewardship does not depend on oversight; it is a genuine value$qb$, $qb$Mostly — resource usage is appropriate but not perfectly consistent$qb$, $qb$Partially — resource management improves noticeably when audit or oversight increases$qb$, $qb$No — unsupervised resource usage is significantly different from supervised behaviour$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S02C10Q04$qb$, $qb$S02$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$When employees observe management behaving in ways that contradict stated organisational values, the typical outcome is:$qb$, $qb$It is surfaced and addressed — contradiction between stated and lived values is taken seriously$qb$, $qb$It is noted privately but not escalated — the inconsistency is tolerated$qb$, $qb$It is ignored — employees have stopped expecting alignment between stated and lived values$qb$, $qb$It is the norm — the gap between stated and lived values is so large that it is not even remarked upon$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S02C10Q05$qb$, $qb$S02$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Management in your organisation maintains commitments to quality standards even when under time or cost pressure:$qb$, $qb$Yes — quality standards are non-negotiable constraints regardless of pressure$qb$, $qb$Mostly — standards are maintained under normal pressure but may slip under extreme conditions$qb$, $qb$Inconsistently — quality standards are frequently traded off against time or cost$qb$, $qb$No — quality is routinely compromised under pressure; standards are aspirational$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S02C10Q06$qb$, $qb$S02$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Management behaviour during periods of organisational difficulty (layoffs, budget cuts, restructuring) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Exemplary — leadership models the values they claim while making hard decisions transparently and compassionately$qb$, $qb$Adequate — decisions are made but communication and compassion are inconsistent$qb$, $qb$Self-protective — management behaviour during difficulty prioritises its own position over organisational values$qb$, $qb$Integrity-destroying — how management behaves during difficult periods is the primary source of trust damage$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S02C10Q07$qb$, $qb$S02$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$When your organisation's interests and a stakeholder's interests conflict, management decisions:$qb$, $qb$Balance both — decisions seek the most mutually beneficial outcome and are made transparently$qb$, $qb$Lean organisational — stakeholder interests are considered but the organisation generally prevails$qb$, $qb$Are made unilaterally — stakeholder interests are given lip service but do not meaningfully affect outcomes$qb$, $qb$Are made cynically — stakeholder interests are used in communications but disregarded in decisions$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S02C10Q08$qb$, $qb$S02$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$When management in your organisation must make a decision that is right for the long term but painful in the short term, they:$qb$, $qb$Make the hard call — long-term integrity over short-term comfort is a visible management value$qb$, $qb$Usually make the right call — but not without significant deliberation and delay$qb$, $qb$Defer — short-term pain avoidance is the dominant management instinct$qb$, $qb$Compromise — the hard right call is routinely traded for the easy wrong one$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S02C10Q09$qb$, $qb$S02$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$When regulatory or legal requirements are costly or inconvenient in your organisation, management:$qb$, $qb$Complies fully — regulatory requirements are met regardless of cost or inconvenience$qb$, $qb$Complies substantially — full compliance is the goal with occasional grey-area management$qb$, $qb$Complies minimally — meeting the letter but not spirit of regulatory requirements is common$qb$, $qb$Cuts corners — non-compliance in the name of commercial expediency is known and accepted$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S02C10Q10$qb$, $qb$S02$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The overall integrity of management in your organisation — measured by the alignment between what is said and what is done — is:$qb$, $qb$High — management in this organisation is known for doing what it says it will do$qb$, $qb$Moderate — integrity is generally maintained but lapses are acknowledged$qb$, $qb$Variable — integrity depends on the specific leader or manager; there is no consistent organisational standard$qb$, $qb$Low — a pervasive integrity gap between management words and actions is a defining feature of this organisation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true);
INSERT INTO question_bank (question_id, section_code, category_code, level_tag, text, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, score_a, score_b, score_c, score_d, active) VALUES
  ($qb$S03C01Q01$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Leaders in your organisation demonstrate awareness of how their behaviour and moods affect the people around them:$qb$, $qb$Yes — leaders are visibly self-aware and adjust their approach based on situational and people context$qb$, $qb$Mostly — most leaders show reasonable self-awareness; some do not$qb$, $qb$Partially — self-awareness is the exception rather than the norm in leadership$qb$, $qb$No — leadership self-awareness is visibly lacking and is a source of organisational dysfunction$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C01Q02$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$When a senior leader's behaviour has negatively impacted their team or colleagues, the typical response is:$qb$, $qb$Acknowledgement and adjustment — the leader recognises the impact and changes their approach$qb$, $qb$Acknowledgement — the impact is recognised but behaviour change is inconsistent$qb$, $qb$Externalisation — the leader attributes the team's response to the team rather than their own behaviour$qb$, $qb$Blindness — leadership behaviour impact on teams is not recognised or acknowledged$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C01Q03$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Senior leaders in your organisation understand and openly acknowledge their own professional limitations:$qb$, $qb$Yes — leaders comfortably name what they are not good at and surround themselves with complementary people$qb$, $qb$Mostly — some leaders acknowledge limitations; others overstate their capability$qb$, $qb$Rarely — admitting limitations is not a comfortable leadership behaviour in this organisation$qb$, $qb$Never — leaders are expected to project capability; acknowledging limitation is seen as weakness$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C01Q04$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Leadership behaviour in your organisation is consistent — not significantly different in public settings versus private ones:$qb$, $qb$Yes — leaders behave the same whether observed or not; authenticity is a defining feature$qb$, $qb$Mostly — occasional inconsistencies are noticed but are not the dominant pattern$qb$, $qb$Partially — significant differences exist between leadership's public and private behaviour$qb$, $qb$No — performance versus reality is a widely understood and discussed leadership dynamic$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C01Q05$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$When leaders in your organisation receive critical feedback about their own leadership, they:$qb$, $qb$Listen and reflect — feedback is received with openness and visibly influences behaviour$qb$, $qb$Receive it but defend — initial defensiveness is followed by some reflection$qb$, $qb$Dismiss it — feedback directed at leadership is typically attributed to the source's limitations$qb$, $qb$Punish it — giving critical feedback to leaders is known to have negative consequences$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C01Q06$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Leaders in your organisation are aware of the impact their stress levels have on team dynamics and manage it visibly:$qb$, $qb$Yes — leaders actively manage their stress and shield their teams from its worst effects$qb$, $qb$Partially — some leaders manage stress well; others pass it down visibly$qb$, $qb$Poorly — leadership stress cascades directly onto teams without management$qb$, $qb$Not at all — leadership emotional dysregulation under pressure is a known morale problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C01Q07$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$Managers in your organisation understand how their communication style lands differently with different people:$qb$, $qb$Yes — managers adapt their communication style to individuals and contexts effectively$qb$, $qb$Some do — communication adaptability varies by manager$qb$, $qb$Few — most managers communicate in one style regardless of the person or context$qb$, $qb$No — communication inflexibility is a known management limitation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C01Q08$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Your organisation invests in developing self-awareness in its leaders (coaching, 360 feedback, psychometric tools):$qb$, $qb$Yes — self-awareness development is a structured part of leadership development$qb$, $qb$Partially — some leaders receive coaching or 360 feedback; it is not universal$qb$, $qb$Rarely — self-awareness development is ad hoc and not a deliberate priority$qb$, $qb$No — investment in leader self-awareness development does not exist$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C01Q09$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The emotional climate set by senior leadership in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Positive and energising — leadership creates a climate of purpose, confidence, and psychological safety$qb$, $qb$Neutral — leadership neither significantly energises nor drains the organisation$qb$, $qb$Tense — leadership creates a climate of anxiety, pressure, and guardedness$qb$, $qb$Toxic — the emotional climate set by leadership is a significant drain on organisational energy$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C01Q10$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$When leaders in your organisation are wrong or make poor judgements, their typical response is:$qb$, $qb$Honest and open — they acknowledge the error and what they would do differently$qb$, $qb$Private acknowledgement — they admit the error to close colleagues but not openly$qb$, $qb$Rationalisation — they reframe the error to make it sound like a reasonable decision$qb$, $qb$Denial — errors in leadership judgement are not acknowledged$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C02Q01$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$When employees in your organisation go through personal difficulty (health, bereavement, family crisis), management responds with:$qb$, $qb$Genuine empathy and practical support — time, flexibility, and care are extended as a matter of course$qb$, $qb$Adequate support — the situation is acknowledged and reasonable accommodation is made$qb$, $qb$Minimal acknowledgement — the situation is noted but the expectation is that work continues normally$qb$, $qb$Indifference — personal difficulties are not considered relevant to the employment relationship$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C02Q02$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$MM$qb$, $qb$Managers in your organisation take time to understand what motivates and matters to each individual on their team:$qb$, $qb$Yes — managers demonstrate genuine knowledge of individual drivers and tailor their approach accordingly$qb$, $qb$Some — the best managers do this; others manage their team as a homogeneous group$qb$, $qb$Few — individual attention is the exception rather than the norm in management practice$qb$, $qb$No — individual needs and motivations are not considered a management responsibility$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C02Q03$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Organisational decisions that affect employees' lives significantly (redundancy, relocation, role changes) are made in your organisation with:$qb$, $qb$Visible consideration of human impact — decisions include support mechanisms and are implemented with care$qb$, $qb$Acknowledgement of impact — the human side is considered but does not always shape the decision or implementation$qb$, $qb$Minimal consideration — business need drives the decision; human impact is managed reactively$qb$, $qb$No consideration — people decisions are made and implemented purely on business grounds$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C02Q04$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$E$qb$, $qb$Employees in your organisation feel that management genuinely cares about them as individuals and not just as resources:$qb$, $qb$Yes — genuine care from management is a visible and consistent feature of the culture$qb$, $qb$Mostly — many employees feel cared for; some do not$qb$, $qb$Partially — care feels performative — present in language but not in behaviour$qb$, $qb$No — employees widely feel they are treated as interchangeable resources rather than people$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C02Q05$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$When performance management action is taken against an employee in your organisation, the process is handled with:$qb$, $qb$Dignity and support — the process is fair, respectful, and includes genuine development support$qb$, $qb$Adequacy — the process follows policy but the human dimension is not always handled well$qb$, $qb$Clinical distance — the process is followed but without meaningful care for the individual$qb$, $qb$Harshness — performance management in this organisation is experienced as punitive, not developmental$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C02Q06$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$When a team is under intense pressure in your organisation, management responds by:$qb$, $qb$Providing visible support — removing blockers, acknowledging effort, and protecting the team from additional load$qb$, $qb$Monitoring — checking in and offering some support but primarily focused on outcomes$qb$, $qb$Adding pressure — increasing oversight and urgency without providing additional support$qb$, $qb$Disappearing — management is least present when teams need them most$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C02Q07$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$When an employee's performance declines, the management instinct in your organisation is to first:$qb$, $qb$Understand — explore what is driving the decline before taking any performance management action$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge — raise the performance concern and open a dialogue before escalating$qb$, $qb$Warn — issue a formal or informal warning before seeking to understand root causes$qb$, $qb$Escalate — move quickly to formal process without significant curiosity about the cause$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C02Q08$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Management in your organisation demonstrates understanding that different employees need different types of support to perform at their best:$qb$, $qb$Yes — differentiated management is visible practice; one-size-fits-all management is not the norm$qb$, $qb$Partially — the best managers differentiate; many do not$qb$, $qb$Rarely — management approaches are uniform regardless of individual differences$qb$, $qb$No — the concept of individualised management is not a practice in this organisation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C02Q09$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$E$qb$, $qb$Employees in your organisation who are struggling (performance, personal, wellbeing) feel safe enough to be honest about it with their manager:$qb$, $qb$Yes — employees know that honesty about struggle will be met with support, not judgement or penalty$qb$, $qb$Depends on the manager — safety to be honest varies by individual manager$qb$, $qb$Rarely — employees manage the impression they give to management rather than sharing genuine struggle$qb$, $qb$No — showing vulnerability to management in this organisation is known to have negative consequences$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C02Q10$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Your organisation tracks and acts on employee wellbeing data (engagement scores, absence trends, mental health indicators):$qb$, $qb$Yes — wellbeing data is collected, reviewed at leadership level, and drives specific management interventions$qb$, $qb$Partially — some data is collected and reviewed but does not consistently drive action$qb$, $qb$Minimally — data is collected for compliance but not actively used to improve management practice$qb$, $qb$No — employee wellbeing is not measured or tracked in any systematic way$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C03Q01$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$When senior leaders in your organisation face high-pressure situations, their emotional composure:$qb$, $qb$Remains stable — leaders are known for staying calm and rational even in extreme circumstances$qb$, $qb$Generally holds — most leaders manage pressure well; occasional lapses occur$qb$, $qb$Is inconsistent — some leaders manage pressure well; others visibly destabilise$qb$, $qb$Is poor — leadership emotional dysregulation under pressure is a significant organisational problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C03Q02$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$When a manager in your organisation is in a bad mood, the impact on their team is:$qb$, $qb$Minimal — managers maintain professionalism regardless of their personal state$qb$, $qb$Noticeable but manageable — some mood transfer occurs but is not severe$qb$, $qb$Significant — team dynamics are visibly affected by manager mood$qb$, $qb$Severe — manager emotional states dominate team climate and are a regular source of anxiety$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C03Q03$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$When challenged or criticised in meetings, leaders in your organisation typically respond with:$qb$, $qb$Openness — challenges are welcomed, engaged with, and the person is respected for raising them$qb$, $qb$Professionalism — the challenge is received without hostility but without full openness$qb$, $qb$Defensiveness — challenges in meetings are met with justification and pushback$qb$, $qb$Hostility — openly challenging leadership in meetings is met with visible displeasure or retaliation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C03Q04$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The overall emotional tenor of leadership interactions with employees in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Energising — interactions with leadership leave employees feeling motivated, valued, and clear$qb$, $qb$Neutral — interactions are professional but neither energising nor draining$qb$, $qb$Draining — interactions with leadership leave employees feeling scrutinised, pressured, or unclear$qb$, $qb$Demoralising — leadership interaction is a primary source of disengagement in this organisation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C03Q05$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$MM$qb$, $qb$When a manager's team makes a significant error, the manager's immediate emotional response is typically:$qb$, $qb$Composed and solution-focused — the manager stays calm, understands what happened, and focuses on resolution$qb$, $qb$Contained frustration — some emotion is expressed but it is managed and quickly refocused$qb$, $qb$Visible frustration — managers frequently display frustration or blame when errors occur$qb$, $qb$Disproportionate — manager emotional responses to errors are feared by teams and damage psychological safety$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C03Q06$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$During periods of uncertainty (market changes, leadership changes, strategic pivots), leadership in your organisation projects:$qb$, $qb$Stability and confidence — leadership transmits a calm, clear, and credible message$qb$, $qb$Professional composure — leadership maintains stability publicly though anxiety may show in smaller settings$qb$, $qb$Visible anxiety — leadership uncertainty is transmitted to the organisation$qb$, $qb$Panic — leadership emotional state during uncertainty amplifies rather than calms organisational anxiety$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C03Q07$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$E$qb$, $qb$Employees in your organisation feel the emotional climate of the workplace is psychologically safe:$qb$, $qb$Yes — the emotional environment is broadly supportive and people feel safe to be themselves at work$qb$, $qb$Mostly — the climate is generally safe with some pockets of difficulty$qb$, $qb$Partially — psychological safety is inconsistent and depends heavily on team and manager$qb$, $qb$No — the emotional climate of the workplace is a significant deterrent to performance and retention$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C03Q08$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Anger, frustration, or emotional outbursts from leadership in your organisation in professional settings:$qb$, $qb$Are extremely rare and addressed when they occur — emotional professionalism is a leadership standard$qb$, $qb$Occasionally occur but are acknowledged and not repeated — not a pattern$qb$, $qb$Occur with some regularity — certain leaders are known for emotional outbursts that are tolerated$qb$, $qb$Are common — emotional aggression from leadership is a feature of the organisational culture$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C03Q09$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$When significant bad news arrives (lost contract, regulatory fine, major error), leadership in your organisation responds by:$qb$, $qb$Staying composed and getting to work — the focus moves quickly to understanding and response$qb$, $qb$Processing and then acting — some initial emotional response followed by structured action$qb$, $qb$Visibly struggling — emotional response dominates and delays productive action$qb$, $qb$Assigning blame — the immediate response to bad news is finding someone responsible rather than solving the problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C03Q10$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$The use of fear, intimidation, or emotional pressure as a management tool in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Is absent — such behaviour is explicitly prohibited and would be immediately addressed$qb$, $qb$Is rare — isolated incidents have occurred but are not a management pattern$qb$, $qb$Occurs in pockets — certain managers or departments are known for this style$qb$, $qb$Is widespread — fear-based management is a defining and damaging feature of this organisation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C04Q01$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$When employees speak to their manager in your organisation, the manager's typical response suggests they have:$qb$, $qb$Genuinely listened — they ask questions, reflect what they heard, and respond to the actual content$qb$, $qb$Heard the main points — the response is broadly relevant but misses nuance$qb$, $qb$Listened partially — the response addresses the manager's agenda more than the employee's concern$qb$, $qb$Not listened — responses are prepared before the employee finishes and do not reflect what was said$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C04Q02$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Leaders in your organisation demonstrate that they understand employee concerns by:$qb$, $qb$Acting on what they hear — employee concerns raised in forums, surveys, or conversations lead to visible change$qb$, $qb$Acknowledging what they hear — concerns are validated but action is inconsistent$qb$, $qb$Summarising what they hear — concerns are noted but not acted on$qb$, $qb$Dismissing what they hear — employee concerns are minimised or ignored$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C04Q03$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$Management in your organisation asks employees for their perspective before making decisions that affect them:$qb$, $qb$Consistently — affected employees are consulted as a matter of standard practice$qb$, $qb$Sometimes — consultation happens for major decisions but not for smaller ones$qb$, $qb$Rarely — decisions affecting employees are made without their input$qb$, $qb$Never — employees are informed of decisions, not consulted before them$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C04Q04$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$E$qb$, $qb$Employees in your organisation feel that when they raise a concern or share a perspective with management, they are genuinely heard:$qb$, $qb$Yes — employees consistently feel heard and see evidence that their input matters$qb$, $qb$Sometimes — feeling heard depends on the manager and the topic$qb$, $qb$Rarely — most employees have stopped expecting to feel heard by management$qb$, $qb$No — the sense that management does not listen is a dominant and damaging employee experience$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C04Q05$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Leadership in your organisation uses structured listening mechanisms (skip-level meetings, town halls with real Q&A, employee surveys with published results) to understand employee reality:$qb$, $qb$Yes — multiple structured listening mechanisms exist and their results visibly influence decisions$qb$, $qb$Partially — some mechanisms exist but are not fully leveraged$qb$, $qb$Minimally — listening mechanisms exist on paper but do not genuinely inform decisions$qb$, $qb$No — there are no structured mechanisms for leadership to hear from employees$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C04Q06$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$MM$qb$, $qb$When an employee brings a problem to their manager, the manager's typical response is to:$qb$, $qb$Explore and understand fully before suggesting any solution$qb$, $qb$Listen and then offer a solution — the solution is relevant but the exploration is brief$qb$, $qb$Jump to solution — the manager responds with their answer before fully understanding the problem$qb$, $qb$Redirect — the manager deflects the problem back to the employee or to HR without engaging$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C04Q07$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$During town halls or all-hands meetings in your organisation, employee questions are:$qb$, $qb$Welcomed and answered honestly — including difficult or critical questions$qb$, $qb$Welcomed but selectively answered — easy questions are addressed; hard ones are deflected$qb$, $qb$Managed — pre-screened or limited to safe topics$qb$, $qb$Discouraged — the format does not genuinely invite real questions$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C04Q08$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Frontline or field employee insights about what is actually happening with customers, operations, or the market reach leadership:$qb$, $qb$Reliably — structured mechanisms exist to surface ground-level insight to leadership regularly$qb$, $qb$Occasionally — insights make it up through informal channels when someone champions them$qb$, $qb$Rarely — the organisational hierarchy filters most ground-level insight before it reaches leadership$qb$, $qb$Almost never — leadership is systematically disconnected from operational reality$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C04Q09$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Listening across cultural, linguistic, or generational differences in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Actively managed — leadership is trained in and demonstrates cultural listening competence$qb$, $qb$Acknowledged — differences are recognised and reasonable accommodation is made$qb$, $qb$Uneven — some groups feel heard; others feel their perspective is systematically missed$qb$, $qb$Poor — cultural and generational listening gaps are a known organisational blind spot$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C04Q10$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Employee engagement survey results in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Taken seriously — results are shared transparently and drive specific, named management actions$qb$, $qb$Reviewed and acknowledged — results are shared and discussed but action is inconsistent$qb$, $qb$Reported but not acted on — results are presented but rarely change management behaviour$qb$, $qb$Managed or suppressed — results that reflect poorly on management are not shared or are minimised$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
