  ($qb$S03C07Q08$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The organisation's culture around disagreement — whether people can disagree professionally and maintain relationships — is:$qb$, $qb$Healthy — disagreement is normal, handled professionally, and does not damage relationships$qb$, $qb$Adequate — most disagreements are managed professionally but some create lasting tension$qb$, $qb$Fragile — disagreement often damages relationships and is therefore avoided$qb$, $qb$Destructive — open disagreement has high personal cost and the culture defaults to conflict avoidance$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C07Q09$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Cross-departmental conflict (over resources, priorities, ownership) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Resolved quickly through clear ownership and effective senior leadership intervention$qb$, $qb$Resolved eventually — cross-departmental conflict is addressed but slowly$qb$, $qb$Persistent — cross-departmental tensions are known and ongoing without satisfactory resolution$qb$, $qb$Unmanaged — cross-departmental conflict is a permanent organisational feature that reduces effectiveness$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C07Q10$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$E$qb$, $qb$Employees in your organisation who experience bullying or persistent interpersonal aggression from colleagues or managers have access to:$qb$, $qb$Effective support and recourse — the process is clear, safe, and outcomes are fair$qb$, $qb$Some recourse — processes exist but effectiveness is inconsistent$qb$, $qb$Limited recourse — processes exist but the personal risk of using them is high$qb$, $qb$No effective recourse — bullying and aggression are unaddressed in this organisation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C08Q01$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Formal recognition programmes in your organisation (awards, employee of the month, performance bonuses) are:$qb$, $qb$Well-designed and valued — criteria are clear, recognition is meaningful, and recipients are genuinely deserving$qb$, $qb$Functional — programmes exist and are used but lack consistency or emotional impact$qb$, $qb$Performative — programmes exist but are not taken seriously by employees or management$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — no formal recognition programmes exist$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C08Q02$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$MM$qb$, $qb$Managers in your organisation give informal, day-to-day recognition (verbal appreciation, personal acknowledgement) to their teams:$qb$, $qb$Regularly and genuinely — specific, timely appreciation is a visible management behaviour$qb$, $qb$Occasionally — recognition happens but is not consistent enough to have real impact$qb$, $qb$Rarely — most managers do not naturally give appreciation and it shows in team morale$qb$, $qb$Never — appreciation from management is almost never experienced by employees$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C08Q03$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$E$qb$, $qb$Employees in your organisation feel their extra effort (working late, going beyond scope, solving difficult problems) is noticed and appreciated:$qb$, $qb$Yes — extra effort is consistently noticed and explicitly acknowledged$qb$, $qb$Sometimes — extra effort is noticed when it is very visible but often goes unacknowledged$qb$, $qb$Rarely — employees frequently go above and beyond without acknowledgement$qb$, $qb$Never — extra effort is expected as standard and is not recognised$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C08Q04$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Recognition in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Equitable and broad — recognition reaches across levels and functions, not just high-visibility roles$qb$, $qb$Generally fair — with some functions or teams receiving more recognition than others$qb$, $qb$Uneven — the same teams and individuals are recognised repeatedly while others feel invisible$qb$, $qb$Extremely narrow — recognition is limited to a small group and is a source of resentment$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C08Q05$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$When a team achieves a major milestone in your organisation, senior leadership:$qb$, $qb$Celebrates it visibly — leadership publicly acknowledges the team's contribution and connects it to organisational success$qb$, $qb$Acknowledges it — formal communication is made but celebration is limited$qb$, $qb$Moves on — milestones are noted but not meaningfully celebrated$qb$, $qb$Ignores it — team milestones pass without leadership acknowledgement$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C08Q06$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$Peer recognition (employees recognising each other's contributions) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Encouraged and visible — peer appreciation is a cultural norm supported by systems and leadership modelling$qb$, $qb$Happens informally — peer recognition exists but is not structured or encouraged$qb$, $qb$Rare — the culture does not naturally encourage peer recognition$qb$, $qb$Non-existent or competitively suppressed — colleagues are unlikely to openly recognise each other$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C08Q07$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$E$qb$, $qb$The type of recognition employees in your organisation most receive is:$qb$, $qb$Personalised and specific — recognition identifies what was done and why it mattered$qb$, $qb$General — appreciation is expressed but without specific connection to the contribution$qb$, $qb$Collective and impersonal — group acknowledgement that does not recognise individuals specifically$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — employees cannot recall receiving meaningful recognition$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C08Q08$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Employees who leave your organisation are recognised for their contribution:$qb$, $qb$Always — departing employees are genuinely appreciated for their service regardless of the circumstances of departure$qb$, $qb$Usually — recognition at departure depends on the relationship and length of service$qb$, $qb$Sometimes — only long-serving or senior employees receive recognition at departure$qb$, $qb$Rarely — exits in this organisation are processed, not celebrated$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C08Q09$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Investment in meaningful employee recognition (financial and time) in your organisation relative to business size is:$qb$, $qb$Proportionate and genuine — recognition investment is visible and valued by employees$qb$, $qb$Adequate — investment exists but is not a strategic priority$qb$, $qb$Minimal — recognition is a low-cost, low-effort activity that reflects limited organisational commitment$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — there is no meaningful investment in employee recognition$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C08Q10$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The overall culture of appreciation in your organisation — the degree to which people feel valued for their work — is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — feeling valued is a consistent and reliable employee experience$qb$, $qb$Moderate — employees feel valued in some contexts and by some managers but not universally$qb$, $qb$Weak — most employees do not feel their work is genuinely valued$qb$, $qb$Very poor — lack of appreciation is a primary driver of disengagement and attrition in this organisation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C09Q01$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Mental health and psychological wellbeing are treated in your organisation as:$qb$, $qb$A strategic priority — investment, policy, and leadership behaviour all reflect genuine commitment$qb$, $qb$An acknowledged responsibility — mental health is on the agenda but not deeply resourced$qb$, $qb$A compliance topic — EAP and policy exist but cultural support is limited$qb$, $qb$Irrelevant — mental health is not acknowledged as an organisational responsibility$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C09Q02$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$Employees in your organisation who are struggling with mental health feel safe enough to be open about it:$qb$, $qb$Yes — mental health disclosure is met with support and does not negatively affect employment$qb$, $qb$Partially — some employees feel safe; others are concerned about stigma or career impact$qb$, $qb$Rarely — the stigma around mental health disclosure is significant$qb$, $qb$No — disclosing mental health challenges in this organisation is known to be career-damaging$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C09Q03$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Workload management in your organisation — whether employees are given sustainable amounts of work — is:$qb$, $qb$Actively managed — managers monitor and balance workload; burnout prevention is a management responsibility$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — most employees have manageable workloads with periodic peak periods$qb$, $qb$Poorly managed — overload is common and is treated as the employee's problem to manage$qb$, $qb$Severely problematic — unsustainable workloads are a known and unaddressed organisational problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C09Q04$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$E$qb$, $qb$The pace and pressure of work in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Stretching but sustainable — the work is demanding but leaves room for quality, recovery, and life outside work$qb$, $qb$Intense but manageable — peaks are high but there are recovery periods$qb$, $qb$Consistently high — sustained high pressure with limited recovery is the normal experience$qb$, $qb$Extreme — the pace of work is a primary driver of health problems, absence, and attrition$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C09Q05$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Managers in your organisation are trained to identify and respond to signs of mental health difficulties in their teams:$qb$, $qb$Yes — mental health awareness and first-aid training is part of the management development programme$qb$, $qb$Partially — some managers have received training; it is not universal$qb$, $qb$Minimally — awareness materials exist but training is not provided$qb$, $qb$No — managers have no training or expectation related to employee mental health$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C09Q06$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Leadership behaviour around work-life boundaries in your organisation (after-hours communication expectations, holiday respect, weekend work norms) is:$qb$, $qb$Healthy — leadership models sustainable work behaviour and protects employee boundaries$qb$, $qb$Mixed — some leaders respect boundaries; others send messages that create implicit pressure$qb$, $qb$Unhealthy — leadership behaviour implicitly or explicitly normalises boundary violations$qb$, $qb$Toxic — boundary violations are expected and not respecting them has career consequences$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C09Q07$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$E$qb$, $qb$Employee Assistance Programme (EAP) or equivalent mental health support in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Known, accessible, and destigmatised — employees are aware of and use support services$qb$, $qb$Available but under-used — support exists but awareness and uptake is low$qb$, $qb$Exists on paper — formal support exists but is effectively inaccessible or unknown$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — no mental health support resources are available to employees$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C09Q08$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Absence related to mental health in your organisation is managed with:$qb$, $qb$The same seriousness and empathy as physical health absence — no stigma or differential treatment$qb$, $qb$General adequacy — mental health absence is managed acceptably though not always with full understanding$qb$, $qb$Some stigma — mental health absence is handled differently from physical health absence$qb$, $qb$Significant stigma — employees actively avoid mental health-related absence for fear of consequences$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C09Q09$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Your organisation measures and tracks employee wellbeing over time and uses the data to make management decisions:$qb$, $qb$Yes — wellbeing metrics are tracked, shared at board level, and drive specific interventions$qb$, $qb$Partially — some wellbeing data is collected and reviewed but does not consistently drive decisions$qb$, $qb$Minimally — wellbeing data is collected for compliance and not actively used$qb$, $qb$No — employee wellbeing is not systematically measured$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C09Q10$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The overall psychological safety of the workplace in your organisation — whether employees feel they can be themselves, make mistakes, and speak up without fear — is:$qb$, $qb$High — psychological safety is a defining feature of the culture$qb$, $qb$Moderate — psychological safety exists in most of the organisation with some exceptions$qb$, $qb$Low — significant psychological safety gaps affect performance and honesty$qb$, $qb$Very low — fear is a dominant feature of the organisational culture$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C10Q01$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Senior leaders in your organisation are visible to employees beyond the C-suite:$qb$, $qb$Yes — leaders regularly interact with employees at all levels; visibility is a deliberate leadership practice$qb$, $qb$Occasionally — visibility happens at formal events but not as a regular practice$qb$, $qb$Rarely — most employees rarely or never interact directly with senior leadership$qb$, $qb$No — senior leadership operates in a separate world from the rest of the organisation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C10Q02$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$Managers in your organisation are accessible to their teams — available for questions, guidance, and support when needed:$qb$, $qb$Yes — managers are known for being genuinely available and responsive$qb$, $qb$Mostly — managers are accessible during normal hours though peak periods reduce availability$qb$, $qb$Partially — some managers are accessible; others are difficult to reach$qb$, $qb$No — managers are widely experienced as inaccessible and unresponsive$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C10Q03$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$When your organisation is going through change or uncertainty, senior leadership increases its presence and communication:$qb$, $qb$Yes — leadership becomes more visible and communicative precisely when it is most needed$qb$, $qb$Somewhat — communication increases but presence is not always visible enough$qb$, $qb$Inconsistently — some leaders increase visibility; others withdraw$qb$, $qb$No — leadership withdraws during uncertainty, leaving a communication vacuum$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C10Q04$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$E$qb$, $qb$The physical and virtual work environments in your organisation enable managers to be meaningfully present with their teams:$qb$, $qb$Yes — work environment design supports genuine manager-team connection regardless of location$qb$, $qb$Mostly — connection is possible but requires deliberate effort in hybrid or distributed settings$qb$, $qb$Partially — the work environment makes manager presence and connection challenging$qb$, $qb$No — remote or distributed working arrangements have effectively eliminated meaningful manager-team presence$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C10Q05$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The leadership team in your organisation is seen by employees as:$qb$, $qb$Real and relatable — employees have a genuine sense of who the leaders are as people, not just as titles$qb$, $qb$Professional but distant — leaders are known but not felt to be genuinely approachable$qb$, $qb$Remote — most employees have limited direct experience of senior leaders$qb$, $qb$Unknown — many employees cannot name their senior leaders or describe their character or style$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C10Q06$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$MM$qb$, $qb$Managers in your organisation conduct regular one-to-one meetings with each of their direct reports:$qb$, $qb$Yes — structured 1:1s are a consistent management practice with clear purpose and follow-through$qb$, $qb$Mostly — 1:1s happen but are sometimes cancelled or lack structure$qb$, $qb$Inconsistently — some managers hold regular 1:1s; others rarely do$qb$, $qb$No — structured 1:1s between managers and their reports are not a management norm$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C10Q07$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Leadership presence during difficult projects or periods in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — leaders are visibly present, engaged, and supportive when teams face difficulty$qb$, $qb$Adequate — leaders check in but their presence is not consistently felt$qb$, $qb$Limited — leadership tends to be present for launches and celebrations but absent during difficulty$qb$, $qb$Absent — difficulty reveals leadership absence as a consistent pattern$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C10Q08$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Walkthroughs, listening tours, or structured senior leader visits to different parts of the business in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Happen regularly and genuinely — leaders learn from these interactions and they visibly influence decisions$qb$, $qb$Happen occasionally — the practice exists but is not frequent enough to have sustained impact$qb$, $qb$Are rare — some leaders do this informally; most do not$qb$, $qb$Do not happen — structured leadership listening to the wider organisation is not a practice$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C10Q09$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$During performance review periods in your organisation, management presence and engagement:$qb$, $qb$Increases meaningfully — managers invest time in genuine review conversations$qb$, $qb$Is adequate — reviews happen but are sometimes rushed or formulaic$qb$, $qb$Is insufficient — performance reviews are treated as an administrative task rather than a genuine engagement$qb$, $qb$Is very poor — performance reviews in this organisation are a compliance activity with minimal real engagement$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S03C10Q10$qb$, $qb$S03$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The overall sense among employees that their leaders know and care about them personally — not just as performers — is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — most employees feel genuinely known and cared for by their leaders and managers$qb$, $qb$Moderate — employees feel professionally managed but not necessarily personally known$qb$, $qb$Weak — most employees feel their leaders see them as resources, not people$qb$, $qb$Very poor — feeling unknown and uncared for by management is a dominant employee experience$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true);
INSERT INTO question_bank (question_id, section_code, category_code, level_tag, text, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, score_a, score_b, score_c, score_d, active) VALUES
  ($qb$S04C01Q01$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The proportion of employees in your organisation who would describe themselves as genuinely happy at work is:$qb$, $qb$High (over 70%) — most employees express genuine satisfaction with their work and workplace$qb$, $qb$Moderate (50–70%) — the majority are satisfied but a significant minority are not$qb$, $qb$Low (30–50%) — less than half the workforce would describe themselves as happy at work$qb$, $qb$Very low (under 30%) — unhappiness is the dominant employee experience$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C01Q02$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Your organisation's most recent employee engagement or satisfaction measurement showed:$qb$, $qb$A score above industry benchmark — and management responded with specific named actions$qb$, $qb$An average score — and some actions were communicated$qb$, $qb$A below-average score — but the response was limited or incomplete$qb$, $qb$Not measured — or the results were not shared and not acted upon$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C01Q03$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$E$qb$, $qb$The dominant emotional experience of employees in your organisation in their day-to-day work is:$qb$, $qb$Motivated and engaged — most employees feel energised by their work and their environment$qb$, $qb$Professionally satisfied — employees are content but not particularly energised$qb$, $qb$Neutral to disengaged — going through the motions describes most employees' experience$qb$, $qb$Unhappy — a significant majority of employees are visibly disengaged or dissatisfied$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C01Q04$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Employee happiness in your organisation is treated as:$qb$, $qb$A business metric — tracked, reported at leadership level, and actively managed$qb$, $qb$An HR responsibility — monitored through surveys but not a leadership priority$qb$, $qb$A nice-to-have — important in principle but not resourced or managed systematically$qb$, $qb$Irrelevant — employee happiness is not a consideration in how the organisation is managed$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C01Q05$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$Employees in your organisation who are unhappy have:$qb$, $qb$Accessible channels to raise concerns and a genuine expectation of being heard and supported$qb$, $qb$Formal channels available but employees are not confident they will make a difference$qb$, $qb$Limited recourse — unhappy employees largely suffer in silence or leave$qb$, $qb$No meaningful recourse — unhappiness is managed through exit, not retention$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C01Q06$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Trends in employee happiness and satisfaction in your organisation over the last 2 years have:$qb$, $qb$Improved — specific initiatives have moved happiness metrics in a positive direction$qb$, $qb$Remained stable — no significant improvement or decline$qb$, $qb$Declined — employee satisfaction has deteriorated and the causes are known$qb$, $qb$Significantly declined — employee happiness is at a critical low and the organisation is in a retention crisis$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C01Q07$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$When employees in your organisation express unhappiness with their work, manager, or organisation, the response from management is:$qb$, $qb$Engaged and curious — management seeks to understand and address the root cause$qb$, $qb$Acknowledged — the concern is noted but action is inconsistent$qb$, $qb$Minimised — unhappiness is often attributed to the employee rather than the environment$qb$, $qb$Ignored or penalised — expressing unhappiness is seen as disloyalty$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C01Q08$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The physical and social work environment (office design, team dynamics, community feel) in your organisation contributes to employee happiness:$qb$, $qb$Positively and significantly — the environment is a deliberate source of wellbeing and community$qb$, $qb$Adequately — the environment is functional and generally pleasant$qb$, $qb$Neutrally to negatively — the work environment does not contribute to wellbeing$qb$, $qb$Negatively — the physical or social environment is itself a source of employee unhappiness$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C01Q09$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Senior leadership in your organisation visibly cares about and prioritises employee happiness:$qb$, $qb$Yes — leadership behaviour and resource allocation demonstrate genuine priority$qb$, $qb$In words — leadership communicates care but this is not always reflected in decisions$qb$, $qb$Weakly — employee happiness is mentioned but is not a real leadership priority$qb$, $qb$No — employee happiness is not a priority in this organisation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C01Q10$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$E$qb$, $qb$If asked whether they would recommend your organisation to a friend as a good place to work, most employees would:$qb$, $qb$Recommend enthusiastically — they are genuine advocates$qb$, $qb$Recommend with caveats — they would flag specific concerns$qb$, $qb$Not recommend — they would not actively endorse the organisation$qb$, $qb$Actively discourage — they would warn friends away from joining$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C02Q01$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Physical health and safety in your organisation's work environment is:$qb$, $qb$Proactively managed — health and safety is a genuine priority with regular assessment and improvement$qb$, $qb$Compliant — legal requirements are met but proactive wellbeing investment is limited$qb$, $qb$Minimal — health and safety is an administrative function rather than a genuine priority$qb$, $qb$Poor — known health and safety issues are unaddressed$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C02Q02$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$E$qb$, $qb$Employees in your organisation have access to adequate physical health support (health insurance, medical leave, sick pay):$qb$, $qb$Comprehensive — health benefits are competitive and accessible to all employees$qb$, $qb$Adequate — standard benefits are in place but not exceptional$qb$, $qb$Minimal — health support is limited to legal requirements$qb$, $qb$Poor — employees lack adequate health support and this is a known retention issue$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C02Q03$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The norms around working hours in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Are healthy and enforced — reasonable working hours are the expectation and leadership models this$qb$, $qb$Are generally healthy — extended hours occur periodically but are not the norm$qb$, $qb$Normalise overwork — extended hours are the implicit expectation for career success$qb$, $qb$Are unhealthy — chronic overwork is a defining feature of the culture with known health consequences$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C02Q04$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$Ergonomics, workspace quality, and the physical conditions of work in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Are actively managed — workspace quality is a visible investment and regularly assessed$qb$, $qb$Are adequate — conditions meet basic standards$qb$, $qb$Are below standard — physical working conditions are a source of employee complaint$qb$, $qb$Are poor — working conditions negatively impact employee health and productivity$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
