  ($qb$S04C06Q08$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Cross-functional relationships between different departments in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Strong — people know and respect each other across functions; collaboration is natural$qb$, $qb$Adequate — cross-functional relationships are professional though not particularly close$qb$, $qb$Weak — silos mean most employees have limited knowledge of or connection with other departments$qb$, $qb$Hostile — cross-functional tension and competition is a dominant organisational feature$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C06Q09$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Investment in team building and social cohesion in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Deliberate and proportionate — team building is a regular practice with measurable impact on cohesion$qb$, $qb$Occasional — some investment is made but not systematically$qb$, $qb$Minimal — team building happens rarely and mostly in response to visible dysfunction$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — no investment is made in team cohesion beyond the work itself$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C06Q10$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Loneliness and isolation as employee experiences in your organisation (particularly in remote or distributed teams) are:$qb$, $qb$Actively managed — the organisation identifies and addresses isolation risk as a management responsibility$qb$, $qb$Acknowledged — isolation risk is recognised and some measures are in place$qb$, $qb$Undermanaged — isolation exists but is not seen as a management problem to solve$qb$, $qb$Severe and unaddressed — significant employee isolation is a known and growing problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C07Q01$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Employees in your organisation feel they have genuine opportunities for career advancement:$qb$, $qb$Yes — career paths are visible, fair, and most employees who perform have real advancement opportunities$qb$, $qb$Partially — advancement is possible for some but the process is not transparent or consistently fair$qb$, $qb$Limited — career advancement in this organisation is slow, opaque, or relationship-dependent$qb$, $qb$Very limited — the absence of career advancement opportunity is a primary driver of attrition$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C07Q02$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$E$qb$, $qb$Employees in your organisation feel their skills are growing and they are becoming more capable over time:$qb$, $qb$Yes — most employees can point to meaningful growth in capability since joining$qb$, $qb$Partially — some employees grow; others feel stagnant$qb$, $qb$Rarely — skill growth is not a common employee experience$qb$, $qb$No — employees leave this organisation with fewer marketable skills than they arrived with$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C07Q03$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Learning and development investment in your organisation (budget, time, programmes) is:$qb$, $qb$Significant and strategic — L&D is a competitive differentiator and a retention tool$qb$, $qb$Adequate — investment exists and is valued by employees$qb$, $qb$Minimal — L&D budget is among the first to be cut and is not a strategic priority$qb$, $qb$Negligible — there is effectively no meaningful L&D investment$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C07Q04$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$MM$qb$, $qb$Managers in your organisation have development conversations with their team members:$qb$, $qb$Regularly — development is discussed at 1:1s, not just performance review time$qb$, $qb$At appraisal time — development is discussed annually but not as a continuous conversation$qb$, $qb$Rarely — most managers do not discuss development unless the employee raises it$qb$, $qb$Never — development conversations between managers and employees do not happen$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C07Q05$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Internal mobility (moving between roles, departments, or locations) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Encouraged and supported — internal moves are facilitated and seen as organisational strength$qb$, $qb$Possible — internal mobility happens but is not actively facilitated$qb$, $qb$Difficult — internal mobility is limited by departmental protectionism or policy$qb$, $qb$Effectively blocked — employees who want to move internally typically leave the organisation instead$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C07Q06$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$E$qb$, $qb$The skills and capabilities employees develop in your organisation make them more valuable in the broader job market:$qb$, $qb$Significantly — working here builds highly transferable skills that are valued externally$qb$, $qb$Partially — some skills developed here are transferable; others are highly specific to the organisation$qb$, $qb$Minimally — the work here does not significantly develop externally valued capability$qb$, $qb$Not at all or negatively — working here does not build career capital$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C07Q07$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Stretch assignments, high-profile projects, and cross-functional opportunities are distributed in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Broadly and fairly — opportunities are accessible to high performers across functions and levels$qb$, $qb$Partially — some employees get regular opportunity; many do not$qb$, $qb$Narrowly — the same small group receives most development opportunities$qb$, $qb$Through relationships — development opportunity allocation is driven by proximity to power, not merit$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C07Q08$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$When an employee in your organisation is ready for promotion, the time between readiness and outcome is:$qb$, $qb$Reasonable — promotion happens within a clear timeframe once readiness is established$qb$, $qb$Somewhat long — promotions happen but the process is slower than employees feel is fair$qb$, $qb$Very long — employees wait years past readiness for promotion$qb$, $qb$Indefinite — promotion timelines are unpredictable and often do not materialise$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C07Q09$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The organisation's track record of developing leaders internally vs. consistently hiring externally for senior roles is:$qb$, $qb$Strong internal development — most senior roles are filled by people who grew within the organisation$qb$, $qb$Balanced — a mix of internal promotion and external hiring$qb$, $qb$External-dependent — most senior hires come from outside, limiting internal career horizons$qb$, $qb$Entirely external — internal career paths to seniority are effectively non-existent$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C07Q10$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The absence of career growth opportunity in your organisation contributes to attrition:$qb$, $qb$Minimally — employees feel they have sufficient growth opportunity and this is not a driver of departure$qb$, $qb$Somewhat — it is a factor for some employees but not the dominant one$qb$, $qb$Significantly — career stagnation is a frequently cited reason for departure$qb$, $qb$Critically — lack of career growth is the primary driver of talent loss in this organisation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C08Q01$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The onboarding experience for new employees in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Structured, warm, and effective — new joiners understand the culture, their role, and are productive quickly$qb$, $qb$Adequate — onboarding covers the basics but is not differentiated or particularly engaging$qb$, $qb$Minimal — onboarding is primarily administrative with limited cultural or professional integration$qb$, $qb$Poor — new employees are expected to figure things out for themselves with minimal support$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C08Q02$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$New employees in your organisation feel productive and confident in their role within:$qb$, $qb$The first 30–60 days — onboarding effectively accelerates productivity$qb$, $qb$60–90 days — integration happens but takes longer than it should$qb$, $qb$3–6 months — slow integration means new hires take a long time to become effective$qb$, $qb$Over 6 months or never — many new employees never feel fully integrated$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C08Q03$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Onboarding in your organisation includes meaningful cultural immersion:$qb$, $qb$Yes — new employees experience and understand the real culture through structured activities and leadership engagement$qb$, $qb$Partially — some cultural orientation is provided but it is not deep or experiential$qb$, $qb$Minimally — onboarding is procedural with limited cultural content$qb$, $qb$No — new employees discover the culture entirely through experience, without guided introduction$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C08Q04$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$MM$qb$, $qb$Buddy or mentoring programmes for new employees in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Structured and effective — new employees are paired with experienced colleagues who actively support integration$qb$, $qb$Available but inconsistent — buddy schemes exist but quality varies$qb$, $qb$Nominal — buddies are assigned but the relationship rarely develops meaningfully$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — new employees have no structured peer support during integration$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C08Q05$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The rate of attrition in the first 12 months of employment in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Low (under 10%) — new hires stay and onboarding effectively retains early talent$qb$, $qb$Moderate (10–20%) — some early attrition exists but is not alarming$qb$, $qb$High (20–35%) — early attrition is a recognised and costly problem$qb$, $qb$Very high (over 35%) — early attrition is a critical business problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C08Q06$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$New employees in your organisation who have a poor onboarding experience:$qb$, $qb$Are identified quickly and given additional support to recover the experience$qb$, $qb$May raise concerns and receive some response$qb$, $qb$Are largely left to adapt without targeted support$qb$, $qb$Leave — the connection between poor onboarding and early attrition is known but not addressed$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C08Q07$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Onboarding quality in your organisation is measured and used to improve the process:$qb$, $qb$Yes — new hire feedback on onboarding is collected, reviewed, and drives consistent improvement$qb$, $qb$Partially — some feedback is collected but improvement is ad hoc$qb$, $qb$Minimally — onboarding is not systematically measured or improved$qb$, $qb$No — onboarding quality is not measured and has not meaningfully changed in years$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C08Q08$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Senior leader engagement with new employees during their first 90 days in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Is structured — leadership connects meaningfully with new employees as a deliberate onboarding element$qb$, $qb$Happens occasionally — some leaders engage with new hires informally$qb$, $qb$Is rare — new employees have limited or no access to senior leadership in their early months$qb$, $qb$Does not happen — senior leaders are invisible to new employees during onboarding$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C08Q09$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$New employees in your organisation who join with high expectations experience:$qb$, $qb$Confirmation of expectations — the reality matches or exceeds what was communicated during recruitment$qb$, $qb$Partial confirmation — some aspects match; others are disappointing$qb$, $qb$Significant gaps — the experience falls noticeably short of expectations set during recruitment$qb$, $qb$Disillusionment — the gap between what was promised and what is experienced is a primary driver of early departure$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C08Q10$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Overall, the onboarding experience in your organisation sets new employees up for:$qb$, $qb$Success — new employees feel set up to perform, belong, and contribute quickly$qb$, $qb$Adequate start — new employees manage but could be better supported$qb$, $qb$Struggle — new employees often spend their early months finding their feet without adequate support$qb$, $qb$Failure — poor onboarding is a significant driver of early performance issues and departure$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C09Q01$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The primary driver of employee engagement in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$A combination of meaningful work, strong management, fair pay, and growth opportunity — multiple genuine drivers$qb$, $qb$Primarily pay and job security — employees stay and perform but are not deeply engaged$qb$, $qb$Inertia — employees stay because leaving is inconvenient, not because they are genuinely engaged$qb$, $qb$There is no clear engagement driver — disengagement is the dominant state$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C09Q02$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The organisation's visibility into the early warning signs of employee disengagement is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — management actively monitors engagement indicators and responds early$qb$, $qb$Moderate — some signals are noticed but response is inconsistent$qb$, $qb$Weak — disengagement is typically only noticed when it becomes attrition$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the organisation consistently fails to notice disengagement until it is too late$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C09Q03$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$Actively disengaged employees in your organisation (those who are checked out and potentially spreading negativity):$qb$, $qb$Are identified early and managed — either re-engaged or exited in a structured way$qb$, $qb$Are sometimes identified and addressed but not consistently$qb$, $qb$Are tolerated — active disengagement is not effectively managed$qb$, $qb$Are widespread and unmanaged — active disengagement is a dominant cultural feature$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C09Q04$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$E$qb$, $qb$The proportion of employees in your organisation who go beyond their minimum requirement (discretionary effort) is:$qb$, $qb$High (over 60%) — most employees regularly go above and beyond what is required$qb$, $qb$Moderate (40–60%) — a significant minority demonstrate discretionary effort$qb$, $qb$Low (20–40%) — most employees do what is required and no more$qb$, $qb$Very low (under 20%) — discretionary effort is rare; minimum effort is the norm$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C09Q05$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$When an employee's performance drops without obvious external cause, management's first response is to:$qb$, $qb$Explore engagement — check in with the employee to understand if there are engagement or personal factors$qb$, $qb$Monitor — observe and allow time before intervening$qb$, $qb$Address performance — raise the performance concern without exploring the engagement dimension$qb$, $qb$Escalate — move quickly to formal process without investigating root causes$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C09Q06$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The connection between manager quality and employee engagement in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Well understood and managed — improving management quality is treated as the primary engagement lever$qb$, $qb$Acknowledged — the link is known but not consistently acted on$qb$, $qb$Underappreciated — management quality is not effectively used as an engagement tool$qb$, $qb$Ignored — the organisation looks for other engagement levers while not addressing management quality$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C09Q07$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$E$qb$, $qb$The sense of pride employees feel in the quality of the work your organisation produces is:$qb$, $qb$High — employees are proud of the quality of products, services, and outcomes they deliver$qb$, $qb$Moderate — employees are satisfied with quality but not proud$qb$, $qb$Low — quality standards are not something employees feel proud of$qb$, $qb$Negative — employees are embarrassed by the quality of output and this is a disengagement driver$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C09Q08$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Annual or periodic engagement surveys in your organisation produce:$qb$, $qb$Actionable insight that drives management change — surveys lead to visible improvements$qb$, $qb$Useful data that is partially acted on — some improvements follow but action is inconsistent$qb$, $qb$Data that is reviewed but rarely drives change$qb$, $qb$Cynicism — employees no longer trust that surveys will lead to improvement and participation is declining$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C09Q09$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The most common reasons employees leave your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Primarily external — better opportunity, life changes; not driven by organisational failure$qb$, $qb$Mixed — a combination of external pull and internal push factors$qb$, $qb$Primarily internal — management quality, culture, growth opportunity, or pay are the dominant departure drivers$qb$, $qb$Internal and known — departure drivers are consistently identified and consistently not addressed$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C09Q10$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Overall employee engagement in your organisation — measured by energy, commitment, pride, and advocacy — is:$qb$, $qb$High — your organisation would score well against global engagement benchmarks$qb$, $qb$Moderate — engagement is above average but not exceptional$qb$, $qb$Below average — engagement is a recognised and partly addressed challenge$qb$, $qb$Very low — disengagement is the dominant employee state and is not being effectively addressed$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C10Q01$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Total compensation (salary, bonus, benefits) in your organisation is perceived by employees as:$qb$, $qb$Fair and competitive — employees feel well-rewarded relative to their contribution and the market$qb$, $qb$Adequate — compensation is acceptable but not a reason to stay$qb$, $qb$Below market — compensation is a recognised retention risk$qb$, $qb$Poor — compensation dissatisfaction is the primary driver of employee attrition$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C10Q02$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The transparency of pay structures and bonus criteria in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — employees understand how pay is determined and what they need to do to earn more$qb$, $qb$Partial — some information is available but full transparency is lacking$qb$, $qb$Low — pay structures are opaque and inconsistency is widely suspected$qb$, $qb$Very low — pay opacity is a significant source of unfairness and resentment$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C10Q03$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$E$qb$, $qb$Non-financial benefits (health, leave, flexibility, learning allowance) in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Comprehensive and valued — employees feel well-supported beyond their base salary$qb$, $qb$Adequate — standard benefits exist and are appreciated$qb$, $qb$Limited — benefits are below market and employees notice the gap$qb$, $qb$Poor — inadequate benefits are a visible retention and attraction problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C10Q04$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Pay reviews in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Regular, fair, and market-aligned — reviews happen on schedule and reflect market rates and performance$qb$, $qb$Regular but imperfect — reviews happen but are not always market-aligned$qb$, $qb$Infrequent — pay is reviewed only when employees push or threaten to leave$qb$, $qb$Reactive — the organisation primarily adjusts pay in response to resignation, not proactive market review$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C10Q05$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$Employees in your organisation feel that high performance is rewarded meaningfully:$qb$, $qb$Yes — high performance leads to tangible financial and non-financial reward$qb$, $qb$Partially — performance is rewarded but the difference between high and average performance is not significant enough$qb$, $qb$Weakly — reward differentiation between performance levels is minimal$qb$, $qb$No — performance and reward are not visibly linked in this organisation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C10Q06$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The organisation's total compensation competitiveness relative to its talent market is:$qb$, $qb$Above market — compensation is a competitive advantage in talent attraction and retention$qb$, $qb$At market — compensation is competitive but not a differentiator$qb$, $qb$Below market — compensation is a known talent acquisition and retention disadvantage$qb$, $qb$Significantly below market — compensation is a critical talent problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C10Q07$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Pay equity (equal pay for equal work regardless of gender, background, or tenure) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Actively managed — pay equity is audited, gaps are identified and closed, and results are communicated$qb$, $qb$Partially managed — some equity analysis is done but not comprehensively$qb$, $qb$Not managed — pay equity is assumed but not measured$qb$, $qb$Known to be poor — pay inequity is a documented or widely suspected problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C10Q08$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$The process for communicating pay decisions (salary, bonus) to employees in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Professional and personal — managers discuss the decision with context, not just a letter or email$qb$, $qb$Adequate — information is communicated with some explanation$qb$, $qb$Impersonal — pay communications are primarily administrative with limited explanation$qb$, $qb$Poor — pay communications are received without context, conversation, or opportunity to ask questions$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C10Q09$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Benefits and total reward programmes in your organisation are reviewed and updated:$qb$, $qb$Regularly against market and employee preferences — programmes evolve to remain relevant and valued$qb$, $qb$Occasionally — reviews happen but not frequently enough to stay consistently competitive$qb$, $qb$Rarely — benefit programmes are largely unchanged and not reviewed against market$qb$, $qb$Never — benefits are static and have not been reviewed in years$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S04C10Q10$qb$, $qb$S04$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Overall, the reward and recognition experience in your organisation makes employees feel:$qb$, $qb$Valued and appreciated — compensation and recognition together reinforce that the organisation values its people$qb$, $qb$Adequately compensated — pay is fair but reward does not make employees feel particularly valued$qb$, $qb$Undervalued — employees feel their contribution is not adequately recognised financially or otherwise$qb$, $qb$Exploited — employees feel they give significantly more than they receive$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true);
INSERT INTO question_bank (question_id, section_code, category_code, level_tag, text, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, score_a, score_b, score_c, score_d, active) VALUES
  ($qb$S05C01Q01$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Hiring in your organisation is driven by:$qb$, $qb$A workforce plan — headcount requirements are forecasted and talent pipelines are built in advance$qb$, $qb$Annual budgeting — headcount is approved annually with some mid-year adjustments$qb$, $qb$Reactive demand — hiring is initiated when a position becomes vacant or urgency forces it$qb$, $qb$Crisis — hiring is typically urgent and unplanned; reactive scrambling is the norm$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C01Q02$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Your organisation's employer value proposition (why talented people should choose to work here) is:$qb$, $qb$Clear, differentiated, and compelling — the organisation knows what makes it attractive and communicates it effectively$qb$, $qb$Defined — an EVP exists but is not consistently or compellingly communicated$qb$, $qb$Vague — the organisation struggles to articulate a clear reason for talented people to choose it$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — no employer value proposition has been defined$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C01Q03$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Diversity targets or inclusion goals in hiring in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Explicit and tracked — diversity hiring goals are set, reported on, and achieving them is a management priority$qb$, $qb$Aspirational — diversity in hiring is a stated goal but not rigorously tracked$qb$, $qb$Mentioned — diversity is referenced in hiring materials but does not meaningfully influence the process$qb$, $qb$Absent — diversity in hiring is not a consideration$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C01Q04$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Critical roles in your organisation have succession or talent pipeline plans that reduce single-point-of-failure dependency:$qb$, $qb$Yes — succession pipelines exist for all critical roles and are actively maintained$qb$, $qb$Partially — some critical roles have succession plans; others do not$qb$, $qb$Minimally — succession is informally considered but not formally planned$qb$, $qb$No — succession planning does not exist and critical role vacancies regularly cause operational crises$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C01Q05$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Talent acquisition is treated in your organisation as:$qb$, $qb$A strategic business function — leadership treats acquiring top talent as a key competitive capability$qb$, $qb$An important HR function — talent acquisition is valued and adequately resourced$qb$, $qb$An administrative function — hiring is seen as a process to be executed rather than a capability to be leveraged$qb$, $qb$A cost centre — talent acquisition is chronically under-resourced and undervalued$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C01Q06$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Your organisation's employer brand (how it is perceived as a place to work in the external market) is:$qb$, $qb$Strong and positive — the organisation attracts unsolicited applications from strong candidates$qb$, $qb$Adequate — the brand is neutral; neither a draw nor a deterrent for most candidates$qb$, $qb$Weak — the employer brand does not help attract quality candidates$qb$, $qb$Negative — known brand problems actively deter strong candidates from applying$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C01Q07$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Future skills planning — identifying the capabilities the organisation will need in 3–5 years — in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Active — future skill needs are mapped and inform both hiring and development strategy$qb$, $qb$Partial — some future skill thinking happens but it is not systematically integrated into hiring$qb$, $qb$Rare — hiring is focused on current needs without consideration of future capability requirements$qb$, $qb$Absent — no structured thinking about future skills exists in this organisation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C01Q08$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$The internal mobility vs. external hiring balance in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Well-calibrated — internal talent is prioritised where appropriate; external hiring fills genuine gaps$qb$, $qb$Partially optimised — internal mobility happens but the balance could be better$qb$, $qb$External-heavy — the organisation tends to hire externally even when internal candidates exist$qb$, $qb$Not managed — there is no deliberate strategy balancing internal and external talent sources$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C01Q09$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The cost and return on investment of hiring in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Tracked and optimised — cost per hire, time to productivity, and quality of hire are measured and improving$qb$, $qb$Partially tracked — cost is monitored but quality and ROI metrics are not systematically measured$qb$, $qb$Not tracked — hiring costs are known but effectiveness is not measured$qb$, $qb$Unknown — there is no measurement of hiring ROI in this organisation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C01Q10$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$External market talent intelligence (salary benchmarks, availability of specific skills, competitor hiring) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Continuously gathered — the organisation stays current on talent market conditions and adjusts strategy accordingly$qb$, $qb$Periodically gathered — market intelligence is collected during active hiring cycles$qb$, $qb$Rarely gathered — market intelligence is sourced reactively when specific needs arise$qb$, $qb$Not gathered — hiring decisions are made without systematic external market intelligence$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C02Q01$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Job descriptions in your organisation before hiring commences are:$qb$, $qb$Accurate, specific, and up to date — they describe real success criteria, not just a list of tasks$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — they cover the main requirements but lack specificity on success criteria$qb$, $qb$Generic — they are cut-and-pasted from similar roles without customisation$qb$, $qb$Absent or meaningless — job descriptions are not used or are not a useful input to hiring$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C02Q02$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The ideal candidate profile (skills, experience, mindset, cultural attributes) for open roles in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Explicitly defined before hiring begins — structured scorecard with weighted criteria is developed per role$qb$, $qb$Informally agreed — the team has a shared view of what good looks like$qb$, $qb$Vague — different interviewers have different and uncollaborated views of the ideal candidate$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — each interviewer brings their own criteria with no common standard$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C02Q03$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$MM$qb$, $qb$Hiring managers in your organisation take accountability for the quality of hires in their teams:$qb$, $qb$Yes — hiring managers own the hiring outcome and are held accountable for hire quality$qb$, $qb$Mostly — hiring managers are involved but accountability is shared with HR to a degree that dilutes ownership$qb$, $qb$Partially — hiring managers delegate significantly to HR and do not feel full ownership$qb$, $qb$No — hiring is seen as HR's job; line managers are not accountable for hire quality$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C02Q04$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Salary bands and compensation for roles in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Market-benchmarked, documented, and consistently applied — transparency reduces negotiation risk$qb$, $qb$Generally defined — salary ranges exist but are not always current or consistently applied$qb$, $qb$Poorly defined — compensation is negotiated case by case without clear structure$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — compensation is entirely ad hoc and leads to significant internal inequity$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C02Q05$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The level (seniority, experience required) specified for open roles in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Accurate and realistic — role levels reflect actual needs and are not systematically over- or under-specified$qb$, $qb$Generally accurate — with occasional over-specification driven by aspiration rather than need$qb$, $qb$Frequently over-specified — roles routinely require more experience than the work actually demands$qb$, $qb$Disconnected from reality — role specifications are driven by internal politics rather than genuine requirements$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C02Q06$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The time taken to define and agree a role before hiring commences in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Short and efficient — role definition is a structured, time-boxed process that takes days, not weeks$qb$, $qb$Moderate — role definition takes a few weeks but eventually produces a usable output$qb$, $qb$Long — role definition is slow and repeatedly revised, delaying hiring start$qb$, $qb$Very long or never completed — hiring starts without a properly defined role in many cases$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C02Q07$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$New hires in your organisation find that their role in practice matches what was described during recruitment:$qb$, $qb$Closely — new hires rarely experience a significant gap between the role as described and as lived$qb$, $qb$Mostly — some differences exist but are not significant enough to affect retention$qb$, $qb$Partially — notable gaps between what was promised and what the role actually involves$qb$, $qb$Significantly — role misrepresentation during hiring is a known driver of early attrition$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C02Q08$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The competency framework or capability model used to define what good looks like at each level in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Clear, current, and consistently used — the framework guides hiring, development, and performance management$qb$, $qb$Exists but inconsistently applied — the framework is available but not uniformly used in hiring$qb$, $qb$Outdated — a framework exists but has not been updated to reflect current business needs$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — there is no defined capability model$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C02Q09$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$When a new hire joins and turns out to be wrong for the role, root cause analysis typically reveals:$qb$, $qb$That the selection process failed — and the process is improved for future hires$qb$, $qb$A specific hiring decision error — but the systemic process is not always adjusted$qb$, $qb$That the role was poorly defined — setting the hire up for failure from the start$qb$, $qb$Nothing — poor hires are managed (or not) without investigation of what went wrong$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C02Q10$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Job architecture (the system of job families, levels, and titles) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Clear and well-maintained — employees understand their position in the structure and what advancement looks like$qb$, $qb$Partly defined — some clarity exists but inconsistencies are acknowledged$qb$, $qb$Inconsistent — job titles and levels are applied without a coherent structure$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — job architecture is chaotic and is a source of confusion and inequity$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C03Q01$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Your organisation's primary sources of hires are:$qb$, $qb$Multiple and diversified — direct sourcing, referrals, agencies, and platforms are all actively managed$qb$, $qb$Two or three channels — sourcing is functional but not fully diversified$qb$, $qb$One or two dominant channels — over-reliance on a single source creates risk and limits talent diversity$qb$, $qb$Primarily reactive job boards — passive, unstrategic hiring approach$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C03Q02$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Referral programmes in your organisation generate:$qb$, $qb$A significant proportion of quality hires — structured referral programme with clear incentives and tracking$qb$, $qb$A useful flow of candidates — referrals are welcomed but the programme is not structured$qb$, $qb$Occasional candidates — referrals happen informally without a managed programme$qb$, $qb$Nothing meaningful — employee referrals are not a functional hiring channel$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C03Q03$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Your organisation's ability to attract passive candidates (people not actively job-seeking) is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — the employer brand, network, and sourcing capability attract candidates who are not actively looking$qb$, $qb$Moderate — some passive candidates are attracted, primarily through networks$qb$, $qb$Weak — hiring is almost entirely dependent on active job seekers$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the organisation has no capability to attract passive talent$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C03Q04$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Campus and early careers recruitment in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Active and structured — campus relationships, internship pipelines, and graduate programmes are managed strategically$qb$, $qb$Present but limited — some campus hiring happens but without a structured programme$qb$, $qb$Ad hoc — early careers hiring happens opportunistically without planning$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the organisation does not engage with early careers talent$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C03Q05$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Time to fill open positions in your organisation (from approval to offer acceptance) is:$qb$, $qb$Fast (under 30 days for most roles) — efficient process means positions are filled quickly without compromising quality$qb$, $qb$Moderate (30–60 days) — reasonable but with room for improvement$qb$, $qb$Slow (60–90 days) — long hiring cycles are causing operational disruption$qb$, $qb$Very slow (over 90 days) — hiring delays are a significant and known business problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C03Q06$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$Candidates who apply to your organisation and are not selected experience:$qb$, $qb$A respectful, timely, and professional process with feedback where possible$qb$, $qb$Adequate communication — candidates are informed of outcomes but the process lacks warmth$qb$, $qb$Poor communication — many candidates are not informed of outcomes in a timely manner$qb$, $qb$Very poor experience — the candidate experience is a known employer brand problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C03Q07$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Social media and digital platforms (LinkedIn, Glassdoor, Indeed) are used in your organisation for talent sourcing:$qb$, $qb$Strategically — active employer branding on digital platforms supports a continuous talent pipeline$qb$, $qb$Functionally — digital platforms are used for posting roles but employer branding is limited$qb$, $qb$Minimally — digital sourcing is not actively managed$qb$, $qb$Not at all — digital talent sourcing platforms are not used$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C03Q08$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Quality of hires from different sourcing channels in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Tracked and analysed — the organisation knows which channels produce the best hires and invests accordingly$qb$, $qb$Generally understood — some knowledge exists about channel quality but it is not rigorously tracked$qb$, $qb$Not tracked — sourcing channel quality is not measured$qb$, $qb$Unknown — the organisation has no insight into which channels produce better or worse hires$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C03Q09$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Talent pools and pipelining (maintaining relationships with strong candidates before roles open) in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Are actively managed — a named talent community exists and is nurtured between hiring cycles$qb$, $qb$Happen informally — recruiters maintain some relationships but there is no structured talent pool$qb$, $qb$Do not happen — hiring is reactive; no proactive pipelining occurs$qb$, $qb$Are not a concept used in this organisation — all hiring starts from zero$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C03Q10$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Glassdoor ratings and external employer reviews of your organisation on hiring platforms show:$qb$, $qb$A positive and improving employer brand — ratings are above industry average and trending positively$qb$, $qb$An average rating — neither notably positive nor negative$qb$, $qb$A below average rating — reviews reflect known internal issues$qb$, $qb$A poor or declining rating — employer brand reviews are a known talent acquisition problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C04Q01$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The interview process in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Structured — standardised questions, scoring rubrics, and multiple interviewers assess against defined criteria$qb$, $qb$Semi-structured — some standardisation exists but the process varies by role and interviewer$qb$, $qb$Unstructured — interviews vary widely; each interviewer does what feels natural$qb$, $qb$Absent — hiring decisions are made on gut feel with minimal formal assessment$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C04Q02$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Interviewer training in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Mandatory and effective — all interviewers are trained in structured interviewing, bias awareness, and scoring$qb$, $qb$Available but optional — training is offered but uptake is inconsistent$qb$, $qb$Minimal — brief guidelines are provided but no meaningful training$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — interviewers receive no training or guidance$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C04Q03$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Unconscious bias in the interview and selection process in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Actively managed — structured processes, diverse panels, and blind screening are used to reduce bias$qb$, $qb$Acknowledged — bias training is provided but structural mitigations are limited$qb$, $qb$Minimally addressed — bias is mentioned but the process does not structurally reduce it$qb$, $qb$Not considered — unconscious bias in hiring is not on the organisational agenda$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C04Q04$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Candidate assessments (skills tests, case studies, psychometrics) in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Role-appropriate and validated — assessment tools are selected for their predictive validity for the specific role$qb$, $qb$Used for some roles — assessments are part of the process for some positions but not consistently$qb$, $qb$Ad hoc — assessment tools are used without clear validation or design$qb$, $qb$Not used — hiring decisions are made from interviews alone without any additional assessment$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C04Q05$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Reference checking in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Structured and genuine — references are checked with specific questions against hire criteria and results influence decisions$qb$, $qb$Done but perfunctory — references are checked but the process is not structured enough to add real value$qb$, $qb$Formality only — references are collected to comply with policy but rarely influence final decisions$qb$, $qb$Not done — reference checking is either not performed or is entirely bypassed$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C04Q06$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$MM$qb$, $qb$Hiring decisions in your organisation are made:$qb$, $qb$By consensus of a diverse interview panel using a structured scoring debrief$qb$, $qb$By the hiring manager with input from others — some structure but hiring manager preference dominates$qb$, $qb$By the most senior person in the room — regardless of who had the most relevant interaction with the candidate$qb$, $qb$By gut feel — structured process gives way to intuition at the point of decision$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C04Q07$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The time a candidate waits between stages in your hiring process is:$qb$, $qb$Short and communicated — candidates receive quick feedback and clear timelines between stages$qb$, $qb$Adequate — delays occur but are within reasonable bounds and are communicated$qb$, $qb$Long — candidates wait too long between stages and strong candidates lose interest$qb$, $qb$Very long and silent — poor candidate experience at this stage regularly costs the organisation strong hires$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C04Q08$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Offer acceptance rates in your organisation (the proportion of offers that candidates accept) are:$qb$, $qb$High (over 85%) — the offer meets expectations set during the process and candidates are well-prepared$qb$, $qb$Moderate (70–85%) — most offers are accepted with occasional losses$qb$, $qb$Below average (55–70%) — a significant number of offers are declined$qb$, $qb$Poor (under 55%) — offer declines are a chronic problem indicating market misalignment or process failure$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C04Q09$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Counter-offer risk (candidates accepting competing offers after accepting yours) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Low — candidates who accept offers join; counter-offers are rarely an issue$qb$, $qb$Moderate — occasional counter-offer losses occur but are not systematic$qb$, $qb$High — counter-offer losses are a frequent and costly hiring problem$qb$, $qb$Very high — the organisation has not addressed the issues that make it vulnerable to counter-offers$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C04Q10$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The predictive validity of the hiring process in your organisation — how well it predicts actual job performance — is:$qb$, $qb$High — hires made through this process consistently perform well; quality of hire metrics are positive$qb$, $qb$Moderate — most hires work out but a notable minority underperform$qb$, $qb$Low — the process does not effectively predict performance and hire quality is variable$qb$, $qb$Very low — a high proportion of hires underperform or exit within 12 months$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C05Q01$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Quality of hire (the performance and retention of people brought in through the hiring process) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Measured and improving — quality of hire is a tracked metric reviewed quarterly$qb$, $qb$Assessed informally — hiring managers give feedback on hire quality but it is not systematically tracked$qb$, $qb$Not measured — quality of hire is not a managed metric$qb$, $qb$Known to be poor — a high proportion of hires underperform or leave within 12 months$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C05Q02$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$New hires in your organisation who turn out to be a poor fit are identified and addressed:$qb$, $qb$Early and constructively — poor fit is identified within 90 days and addressed through coaching or exit$qb$, $qb$Eventually — poor fit is identified within 6 months but the process is slow$qb$, $qb$Late — poor hires persist for a year or more before being formally addressed$qb$, $qb$Never or too late — poor hires are tolerated indefinitely or only addressed when they become a crisis$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C05Q03$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The time it takes for a new hire in your organisation to become fully productive is:$qb$, $qb$Short (under 90 days for most roles) — effective onboarding accelerates time to productivity$qb$, $qb$Moderate (90–180 days) — reasonable but with room for improvement$qb$, $qb$Long (6–12 months) — slow productivity ramp is a known challenge$qb$, $qb$Very long (over 12 months) — new hire productivity takes too long and represents a significant cost$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C05Q04$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The proportion of new hires who meet or exceed performance expectations in their first year in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High (over 80%) — the hiring process reliably identifies people who perform well$qb$, $qb$Moderate (65–80%) — most hires perform but a meaningful minority do not$qb$, $qb$Below average (50–65%) — more than a third of new hires disappoint$qb$, $qb$Low (under 50%) — hiring quality is so poor that more hires disappoint than succeed$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C05Q05$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The proportion of new hires who leave within their first 12 months in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Low (under 10%) — the organisation retains most new hires through good hiring and onboarding$qb$, $qb$Moderate (10–20%) — some early attrition but not alarming$qb$, $qb$High (20–35%) — early attrition is a significant and costly problem$qb$, $qb$Very high (over 35%) — first-year attrition is a critical business risk$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C05Q06$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Hiring managers in your organisation receive feedback on the quality of hires they have made:$qb$, $qb$Regularly — hiring managers receive structured quality of hire feedback and use it to improve their hiring decisions$qb$, $qb$Occasionally — some feedback is provided but not systematically$qb$, $qb$Rarely — hiring managers do not receive formal feedback on hire quality$qb$, $qb$Never — there is no feedback loop between hire outcomes and future hiring decisions$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C05Q07$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The cost of a poor hire (recruitment, onboarding, lost productivity, attrition) is understood and tracked in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Yes — the cost of a bad hire is quantified and used as a business case for improving hiring quality$qb$, $qb$Approximately — leaders understand the cost is significant but have not quantified it$qb$, $qb$Vaguely — the cost is acknowledged in principle but not understood or tracked$qb$, $qb$No — the cost of poor hiring is not understood or tracked$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C05Q08$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Cultural fit assessment (whether candidates will thrive in the organisation's culture) in the hiring process is:$qb$, $qb$Structured and balanced — culture fit is assessed against specific observable behaviours, not vague feeling$qb$, $qb$Considered but informal — culture fit is assessed but the criteria are not explicit$qb$, $qb$Overweighted — culture fit (likability) dominates selection in a way that reduces diversity$qb$, $qb$Not assessed — culture fit is not considered in the selection process$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C05Q09$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$Managers in your organisation who make repeated poor hires are:$qb$, $qb$Supported and developed — the pattern is identified and the manager receives coaching on hiring quality$qb$, $qb$Flagged — repeated poor hire patterns are noticed but response is inconsistent$qb$, $qb$Tolerated — repeated poor hiring is not addressed at management level$qb$, $qb$Not tracked — there is no mechanism to identify repeated poor hiring patterns$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C05Q10$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Hiring for senior and critical roles in your organisation involves:$qb$, $qb$External expertise (search firms, assessment centres, board oversight) as well as structured internal process$qb$, $qb$A more rigorous version of the standard process with additional stakeholder involvement$qb$, $qb$The same process as mid-level hiring — no additional rigour for critical roles$qb$, $qb$Less rigour — senior hiring is often faster and less structured than mid-level hiring$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C06Q01$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Approval processes for new headcount in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Fast and clear — headcount approval takes days, not weeks, through a defined process$qb$, $qb$Moderate — approval takes 2–4 weeks through a reasonably clear process$qb$, $qb$Slow — headcount approval is a bureaucratic process that delays hiring by weeks or months$qb$, $qb$Very slow or broken — headcount approval is an unpredictable and chronic business problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C06Q02$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Hiring manager involvement in the recruitment process in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Active and timely — hiring managers respond to CVs, schedule interviews, and give feedback quickly$qb$, $qb$Adequate — hiring managers are involved but sometimes slow to engage$qb$, $qb$Insufficient — hiring manager delays are a consistent bottleneck in the process$qb$, $qb$Very poor — hiring managers are disengaged and delegate almost entirely to HR, then complain about hire quality$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C06Q03$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The number of interview rounds in your organisation for typical positions is:$qb$, $qb$Optimal — enough to make a good decision without wasting candidate or interviewer time$qb$, $qb$Slightly excessive — one more round than necessary but manageable$qb$, $qb$Too many — over-engineering the interview process loses good candidates$qb$, $qb$Chaotic — the number of rounds varies unpredictably and is not designed for candidate experience$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C06Q04$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The use of technology (ATS, video interviewing, AI screening) to improve hiring efficiency in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Sophisticated and effective — technology reduces admin burden, speeds processes, and improves candidate experience$qb$, $qb$Partial — an ATS is in use but the full potential of technology is not leveraged$qb$, $qb$Minimal — limited technology is in use; most processes are manual$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — hiring is entirely manual with no supporting technology$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C06Q05$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The efficiency of the HR/recruiting function in your organisation in terms of hiring speed relative to quality is:$qb$, $qb$High — the function consistently delivers quality hires within competitive timeframes$qb$, $qb$Adequate — delivery is reliable but not highly efficient$qb$, $qb$Slow — the recruiting function is a known bottleneck$qb$, $qb$Dysfunctional — recruiting function performance is a significant and unresolved business problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C06Q06$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$Candidates who interview with your organisation report the process is:$qb$, $qb$Professional, respectful, and well-organised — a positive brand experience regardless of outcome$qb$, $qb$Adequate — professional enough but not particularly impressive$qb$, $qb$Disorganised — candidates experience unclear processes, late responses, or poor coordination$qb$, $qb$Very poor — candidate experience is a known employer brand problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C06Q07$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Hiring metrics (time to fill, cost per hire, acceptance rate, quality of hire) are tracked and reviewed in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Monthly at leadership level — metrics drive continuous improvement in the hiring function$qb$, $qb$Periodically — some metrics are reviewed but not comprehensively or frequently$qb$, $qb$Rarely — metrics are collected but not reviewed or used to drive improvement$qb$, $qb$Never — hiring metrics are not tracked in this organisation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C06Q08$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The experience of the hiring process for internal candidates in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Positive and fair — internal candidates are given proper consideration and receive feedback regardless of outcome$qb$, $qb$Adequate — internal candidates are considered though the process is not always smooth$qb$, $qb$Poor — internal candidates feel the process is unfair or opaque$qb$, $qb$Discouraging — the internal hiring experience causes employees to apply externally instead$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C06Q09$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Job offers in your organisation are extended:$qb$, $qb$Promptly after decision — offers go out within 24–48 hours of the hiring decision$qb$, $qb$Within a week — acceptable but occasionally risks losing candidates$qb$, $qb$After a long delay — offer processes are slow and cost the organisation candidates$qb$, $qb$Very slowly — offer delays are a regular cause of candidate loss$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C06Q10$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The hiring process for volume roles (multiple hires of the same type) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Efficient and scalable — volume hiring is managed through structured assessment and efficient processes$qb$, $qb$Managed — volume hiring is handled but is resource-intensive$qb$, $qb$Strained — volume hiring stretches the organisation's capacity significantly$qb$, $qb$Broken — volume hiring is a recurring operational crisis$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C07Q01$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The use of external recruitment agencies in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strategic and managed — preferred supplier agreements with clear KPIs; agency use is controlled and cost-effective$qb$, $qb$Functional — agencies are used and relationships exist but management is not strategic$qb$, $qb$Ad hoc — multiple agencies are used without coordination, leading to duplication and inflated costs$qb$, $qb$Unmanaged — agency usage is uncontrolled and a significant and known cost problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C07Q02$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The quality of candidates presented by external agencies to your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Consistently high — agencies understand the brief well and present relevant, quality candidates$qb$, $qb$Variable — some agencies consistently deliver; others do not$qb$, $qb$Generally poor — agencies regularly present misaligned or low-quality candidates$qb$, $qb$Very poor — agency candidates rarely meet the quality standard and the expense is not justified$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C07Q03$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The proportion of hires made through external agencies vs. direct sourcing in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Optimised — direct sourcing is the primary channel; agencies are used for genuinely hard-to-fill roles$qb$, $qb$Balanced — agencies and direct sourcing are both significant channels$qb$, $qb$Agency-heavy — the organisation is over-reliant on agencies for routine hiring$qb$, $qb$Almost entirely agency — direct sourcing capability does not exist$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C07Q04$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Agency recruitment fees as a proportion of total hiring spend in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Low (under 15% of total hiring spend) — direct sourcing reduces agency dependency$qb$, $qb$Moderate (15–25%) — fees are significant but managed$qb$, $qb$High (25–40%) — agency fees are a significant and poorly managed cost$qb$, $qb$Very high (over 40%) — agency over-reliance is a significant and unaddressed financial problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C07Q05$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Briefing quality provided by your organisation to external agencies is:$qb$, $qb$Detailed and specific — agencies receive role-specific briefs with culture context and success criteria$qb$, $qb$Adequate — basic information is provided but briefs lack depth$qb$, $qb$Poor — agencies receive minimal briefing and must interpret the requirement$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — agencies are given a job description and nothing more$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C07Q06$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Agency performance is tracked and under-performing agencies are replaced in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Yes — agency performance is tracked against KPIs and under-performers are exited from the PSL$qb$, $qb$Partially — performance is informally monitored and some agencies are replaced over time$qb$, $qb$Rarely — agency relationships persist regardless of performance$qb$, $qb$Never — no systematic agency performance management exists$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C07Q07$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Hiring costs associated with recruitment in your organisation (agency fees, advertising, assessment tools) are:$qb$, $qb$Tracked and benchmarked — cost per hire is known and compared against industry benchmarks$qb$, $qb$Approximately tracked — costs are monitored but not rigorously benchmarked$qb$, $qb$Poorly tracked — total hiring costs are not fully understood$qb$, $qb$Unknown — there is no systematic tracking of total recruitment costs$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C07Q08$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Executive search for senior leadership roles in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Managed through experienced, specialist firms with structured briefs and rigorous shortlisting$qb$, $qb$Conducted through reputable firms with adequate process$qb$, $qb$Managed through generalist agencies without specialist senior-hire expertise$qb$, $qb$Non-existent or self-managed — senior hiring is done without specialist external support$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C07Q09$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The employer brand your organisation presents through agencies to the external talent market is:$qb$, $qb$Consistent with internal reality — agencies present an accurate and compelling employer brand$qb$, $qb$Generally consistent — some over-selling occurs but not systematically$qb$, $qb$Over-sold — agencies present the organisation more favourably than new hires experience$qb$, $qb$Damaged — agency behaviour or misrepresentation is harming the external employer brand$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C07Q10$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The transition from external agency to building internal direct sourcing capability in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$A stated strategy being actively delivered — internal capability is growing and agency dependency is reducing$qb$, $qb$Acknowledged as a goal — some movement toward direct sourcing but without a structured plan$qb$, $qb$Not prioritised — agency reliance is accepted as the long-term model$qb$, $qb$Not considered — the organisation has no awareness of or plan to address agency over-reliance$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C08Q01$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Diverse candidate slates (ensuring candidates from underrepresented groups are included in every shortlist) are:$qb$, $qb$A formal policy — no shortlist is approved without diverse representation$qb$, $qb$An aspiration — diversity in shortlists is encouraged but not enforced$qb$, $qb$Mentioned but not enforced — lip service to diversity exists without structural enforcement$qb$, $qb$Not considered — shortlist diversity is not a hiring practice in this organisation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C08Q02$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The proportion of women in senior leadership roles in your organisation has over the last 3 years:$qb$, $qb$Increased meaningfully — specific initiatives have moved representation in the right direction$qb$, $qb$Remained stable at an acceptable level$qb$, $qb$Remained stable at an insufficient level — acknowledged gap with limited progress$qb$, $qb$Declined or was never adequate — gender representation at senior level is a known and unaddressed failure$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C08Q03$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Structured interviews and standardised scoring in your organisation are used to:$qb$, $qb$Reduce bias and improve consistency — all interviewers use the same questions and scoring rubric$qb$, $qb$Guide the process — some standardisation exists but scoring is not consistently applied$qb$, $qb$Provide a loose framework — standardisation exists on paper but interviewers diverge in practice$qb$, $qb$Not used — interviews are entirely unstructured with no bias-reduction mechanism$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C08Q04$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Pay equity analysis for new hires (ensuring no pay discrimination by gender, background, or protected characteristic) is:$qb$, $qb$Embedded in the offer process — all offers are reviewed for equity before issue$qb$, $qb$Conducted periodically — equity is reviewed annually but not at point of hire$qb$, $qb$Aspirational — equity is a stated value but not built into the hiring process$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — pay equity at point of hire is not considered$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C08Q05$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Inclusion in the hiring process — making the process accessible and welcoming for candidates with different backgrounds, disabilities, or needs — is:$qb$, $qb$Actively managed — reasonable adjustments are offered proactively; accessibility is designed into the process$qb$, $qb$Available on request — accommodations are made when candidates ask$qb$, $qb$Inconsistent — some adjustments are made but without a consistent approach$qb$, $qb$Absent — the hiring process does not accommodate diverse candidate needs$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C08Q06$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Unconscious bias training for people involved in hiring decisions in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Mandatory and current — all interviewers and decision-makers receive regular bias training$qb$, $qb$Available — training exists but is not mandatory and uptake is inconsistent$qb$, $qb$Nominal — a single, dated training module exists without meaningful reinforcement$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — bias training for hiring decision-makers has not been provided$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C08Q07$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The diversity of your organisation's hiring panel (who participates in interviews and hiring decisions) is:$qb$, $qb$Deliberately managed — panels include diverse members and the diversity of the panel is seen as important$qb$, $qb$Partially managed — some panels are diverse; others are homogeneous$qb$, $qb$Not managed — panel diversity is not considered in hiring process design$qb$, $qb$Poor — hiring panels are consistently homogeneous and this is known to reinforce bias$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C08Q08$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Candidates from non-traditional educational or professional backgrounds in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Actively considered — the organisation deliberately removes credential barriers that do not predict performance$qb$, $qb$Welcome in principle — non-traditional backgrounds are accepted but screening may still filter them out$qb$, $qb$Disadvantaged — credential requirements disadvantage non-traditional candidates in practice$qb$, $qb$Excluded — the organisation's hiring criteria systematically screen out non-traditional backgrounds$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C08Q09$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The measurement and reporting of diversity hiring metrics in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Transparent and regular — diversity metrics are reported at board level and to employees$qb$, $qb$Periodic and internal — metrics are tracked internally but not widely shared$qb$, $qb$Minimal — some data is collected but not reported or acted on systematically$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — diversity in hiring is not measured$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C08Q10$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C08$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The organisation's overall commitment to diverse and inclusive hiring — measured by outcomes rather than stated intentions — is:$qb$, $qb$Demonstrable — diversity of representation has improved through sustained, structural change$qb$, $qb$Genuine but slow — the organisation is committed but progress is gradual$qb$, $qb$Aspirational — commitment is stated but outcomes have not meaningfully changed$qb$, $qb$Performative — diversity commitments are made publicly without structural change or accountability$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C09Q01$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Values alignment assessment in the hiring process in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Structured and specific — values-based interview questions are used with defined behavioural indicators$qb$, $qb$Present but informal — values are discussed in interviews but without structured assessment$qb$, $qb$Superficial — values alignment is mentioned but not meaningfully assessed$qb$, $qb$Absent — values are not assessed in the hiring process$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C09Q02$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The risk of hiring for cultural similarity (finding people like those already here) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Actively managed — culture fit assessment distinguishes between values alignment and cultural conformity$qb$, $qb$Partially managed — the risk is acknowledged but not structurally mitigated$qb$, $qb$Unmanaged — culture fit assessment tends toward finding people similar to current employees$qb$, $qb$A dominant problem — cultural conformity is a major bias in this organisation's hiring$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C09Q03$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Candidates who accept offers in your organisation have:$qb$, $qb$A clear and accurate understanding of the culture and work environment — set up for realistic expectations$qb$, $qb$A reasonable understanding — some aspects are well-communicated; others are unclear$qb$, $qb$A partial understanding — the culture presented during hiring differs from reality$qb$, $qb$A misleading understanding — culture is oversold and new hires regularly feel misled$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C09Q04$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$New hires who leave within their first 90 days in your organisation most often cite as the reason:$qb$, $qb$External factors — their circumstances changed; cultural or role misalignment is not the primary cause$qb$, $qb$Role mismatch — the role turned out different from what was expected$qb$, $qb$Cultural mismatch — the culture was different from what was communicated during hiring$qb$, $qb$Management problems — the direct management relationship is the primary early-departure driver$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C09Q05$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Candidates are given an honest preview of the challenges and realities of working in your organisation during the hiring process:$qb$, $qb$Yes — a realistic job preview is a deliberate part of the hiring process to reduce early attrition$qb$, $qb$Partially — some honesty is shared but the organisation still tends to project its best self$qb$, $qb$Minimally — challenges are not disclosed and candidates are recruited on the basis of an idealistic picture$qb$, $qb$No — the hiring process sells the organisation without any disclosure of real challenges$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C09Q06$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The values and behaviours that define success in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Clearly defined and used to screen candidates — people who embody the values get hired; those who don't don't$qb$, $qb$Defined and referenced in hiring — values are discussed but not used to make a decisive selection difference$qb$, $qb$Defined but not operationalised in hiring — values exist but don't feature meaningfully in selection$qb$, $qb$Not defined — there are no clear values-based criteria for selection$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C09Q07$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$After a new hire leaves within 6 months citing cultural mismatch, your organisation's response is to:$qb$, $qb$Review the hiring process — specifically assess whether cultural assessment was adequate and what can be improved$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the loss — but process improvement is not systematic$qb$, $qb$Attribute it to the individual — cultural mismatch is seen as a personal failure, not a process failure$qb$, $qb$Ignore it — early cultural exits are not analysed for process improvement$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C09Q08$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$MM+E$qb$, $qb$Existing employees are involved in cultural assessment during the hiring process in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Deliberately — peer interviews or culture interviews are a structured part of the process$qb$, $qb$Occasionally — some roles include peer interaction during the hiring process$qb$, $qb$Rarely — hiring is largely conducted by managers and HR without peer involvement$qb$, $qb$Never — existing employees have no role in cultural assessment during hiring$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C09Q09$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Hiring for potential (capability and growth trajectory) vs. purely for current fit in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Balanced — the organisation deliberately seeks to hire for both current capability and future potential$qb$, $qb$Partially balanced — potential is considered but current fit dominates$qb$, $qb$Fit-focused — hiring criteria are entirely about the current role with little consideration of future potential$qb$, $qb$Short-sighted — the organisation hires for immediate task delivery with no consideration of growth trajectory$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C09Q10$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Hiring decisions that prioritise speed over quality in your organisation (filling a vacancy quickly at the expense of finding the right person) are:$qb$, $qb$Rare and acknowledged as a mistake when they happen — quality is defended even under time pressure$qb$, $qb$Occasional — speed sometimes compromises quality but is not the dominant pattern$qb$, $qb$Common — the pressure to fill vacancies frequently produces quality compromises$qb$, $qb$The norm — speed is the primary hiring metric and quality is routinely sacrificed$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C10Q01$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Your organisation's use of AI in talent acquisition (CV screening, candidate matching, interview scheduling) is:$qb$, $qb$Active and improving — AI tools are deployed, monitored for bias, and improving hiring efficiency$qb$, $qb$Exploring — some AI tools are in use or being evaluated$qb$, $qb$Not yet adopted — awareness exists but no deployment$qb$, $qb$Non-existent or unaware — AI in hiring is not on the agenda$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C10Q02$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Data analytics for hiring decisions (using data to improve sourcing, assessment, and retention) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Sophisticated — people analytics drives continuous improvement in hiring strategy$qb$, $qb$Basic — some data is used (time to fill, source of hire) but analytics is limited$qb$, $qb$Minimal — data is collected but not used for strategic decision-making$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — hiring decisions are not informed by data analytics$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C10Q03$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The hiring strategy for skills that are increasingly automated (roles that AI may displace) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Proactive — the organisation is mapping automation risk and adjusting its hiring strategy accordingly$qb$, $qb$Aware — the issue is acknowledged and some thinking is happening$qb$, $qb$Reactive — automation impact will be dealt with when it forces a response$qb$, $qb$Unaware — the potential automation of current roles is not being considered in hiring planning$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C10Q04$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Hiring for digital and AI-related skills in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$A strategic priority — digital and AI skill is assessed in all relevant roles and developed across the organisation$qb$, $qb$Growing — digital skills are increasingly valued in hiring but not yet systematically assessed$qb$, $qb$Limited — digital skills are valued for specialist roles but not considered in general hiring$qb$, $qb$Not happening — digital and AI skills are not a feature of the hiring strategy$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C10Q05$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Video and remote interviewing in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Well-integrated — structured, consistent, and providing a positive candidate experience$qb$, $qb$Functional — video interviews are used but the process is not fully optimised$qb$, $qb$Basic — video interviews are used but without structure or process quality$qb$, $qb$Not established — the organisation has not effectively integrated remote interviewing$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C10Q06$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The Applicant Tracking System (ATS) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Modern, widely adopted, and actively improving recruiter and candidate experience$qb$, $qb$Functional but not fully utilised — the system has more capability than the organisation uses$qb$, $qb$Outdated — the ATS is a source of frustration for recruiters and candidates$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — no ATS is in use$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C10Q07$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Skills-based hiring (assessing actual capability rather than credentials and experience as a proxy) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$A growing practice — skills assessments and work samples are used to evaluate actual capability$qb$, $qb$Partially adopted — skills assessment is used for some roles but not broadly$qb$, $qb$Rare — credentials and experience remain the primary selection criteria$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the organisation has not moved toward skills-based hiring$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C10Q08$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Gig economy and freelance talent as a sourcing option in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Actively used — contingent workforce is a planned component of the talent strategy$qb$, $qb$Used ad hoc — freelancers are hired when needed but without strategic planning$qb$, $qb$Rarely used — the organisation defaults to permanent hiring even when flexible arrangements would serve better$qb$, $qb$Not used — the contingent workforce is not part of the talent strategy$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C10Q09$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The organisation's readiness to hire and integrate talent from geographically distributed or fully remote locations is:$qb$, $qb$High — the organisation has infrastructure, process, and culture to hire and integrate remote talent effectively$qb$, $qb$Moderate — some remote hiring capability exists but it is not fully developed$qb$, $qb$Limited — the organisation struggles to effectively hire and integrate remote employees$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the organisation only hires for on-site roles$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S05C10Q10$qb$, $qb$S05$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Overall readiness of the talent acquisition function in your organisation to attract and hire the workforce of the next 5 years is:$qb$, $qb$High — the function is modern, data-driven, and strategically aligned to future talent needs$qb$, $qb$Developing — the function is improving but not yet at the level required$qb$, $qb$Behind — the function is using yesterday's tools and approaches for tomorrow's talent market$qb$, $qb$Very low — the talent acquisition function needs significant structural investment and modernisation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true);
